ints on that row.  \nThe vertical and slope \\(-1\\) cases are analogous. ∎\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+n = 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (2n-1)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-4n+2}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-3n+2}{2}\n=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot cover all points of \\(S\\), contradicting the first requirement of the problem.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq n-1}\n\\]\n\nThis establishes a necessary restriction on the admissible values of \\(k\\). The determination of all admissible \\(k\\) (in particular, whether every \\(k\\le n-2\\) and \\(k=n\\) can be realized) remains open."
