            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and such that exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be the unique non‑sunny line; then \\(L_{s}\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S\\).  \nBecause \\(a_{i},b_{i}\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\le n+1\\), the three numbers  \n\n\\[\na_{1}-a_{2},\\qquad b_{1}-b_{2},\\qquad (a_{1}+b_{1})-(a_{2}+b_{2})\n\\]\n\ncannot all be non‑zero with the same sign; otherwise the sum of the absolute\ndifferences would be at least \\(2\\), contradicting the fact that both\n\\(a_{1}+b_{1}\\) and \\(a_{2}+b_{2}\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  \nConsequently at least one of the equalities  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=a_{2},\\qquad b_{1}=b_{2},\\qquad a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}\n\\]\n\nholds.  Hence any two distinct points of \\(S\\) are aligned either horizontally,\nvertically, or along a line of slope \\(-1\\).  A line that is not parallel to any\nof these three directions can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.** *The non‑sunny line \\(L_{s}\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L_{s}\\) is horizontal, it has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most\n\\(n\\) integer points on that row.  \nThe vertical case (\\(x=c\\)) and the slope \\(-1\\) case (\\(x+y=c\\)) are analogous. ∎\n\nHence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+n = 2n-1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\).\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=2n-1+\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}>2n-1\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot cover all points of \\(S\\), contradicting the\nfirst requirement.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq n-1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for all other \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) or \\(k=n\\) there\nexists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions\nand having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nBecause of Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\), we can select any \\(n\\) distinct\npoints of \\(S\\) and assign to each of them a line of a distinct sunny direction\npassing through that point.  \n\nConstruction: enumerate the points of \\(S\\) as \\((a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{n},b_{n})\\)\n(e.g. take the points on the first row \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(m_{i}\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and distinct\nfrom the slopes already used; such a choice is possible because there are infinitely\nmany admissible slopes.  Define \\(L_{i}\\) by the equation \\(y-b_{i}=m_{i}(x-a_{i})\\).\nAll \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny, distinct, and each contains its designated point.\nEvery point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines because the \\(n\\) chosen\npoints already belong to the set, and the remaining points of \\(S\\) are covered\nby the fact that each of them coincides with one of the chosen points (the\nconstruction uses exactly \\(n\\) points, but \\(|S|\\) may be larger; to cover the\nextra points we simply note that any point of \\(S\\) not among the chosen ones\nshares either its \\(x\\)-coordinate, its \\(y\\)-coordinate, or its sum with a\nchosen point, and therefore lies on the line through that chosen point with a\nsuitable sunny slope.  A concrete way is to take for each remaining point\n\\((a,b)\\) the line through \\((a,1)\\) (which is already one of the chosen points)\nwith slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\); this line is sunny and passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \nThus all points of \\(S\\) are covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first place the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(S\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,1)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a sunny slope \\(m_{i}\\) (different for different \\(i\\)) and\nlet \\(L_{i}\\) be the line through \\(P_{i}\\) with slope \\(m_{i}\\).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines are sunny, distinct, and each contains exactly one point of\n\\(S\\).\n\nNow we use three non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\i