of distinctness and coverage.**  \n   Care is taken to choose the slopes of the sunny lines so that no two of them coincide and none of them becomes parallel to the forbidden directions. The added forbidden line is always chosen to be either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\) and is distinct from the existing lines because it passes through a point not lying on any previous line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By induction the lemma holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nWe shall prove:\n\n\\[\n\\forall n\\ge 3,\\;\\forall k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;\\exists\\; \\mathcal L_{n,k}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following four families of three lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines have forbidden slopes, and together they cover \\(T_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac52 .\n  \\]\n  The third line has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); it passes through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n  \\ell_2:\\;y= -\\tfrac13 x+ \\tfrac43 ,\\qquad \n  \\ell_3:\\;y= -\\tfrac23 x+ \\tfrac53 .\n  \\]\n  Both \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) are sunny and together with the horizontal line \\(\\ell_1\\) they cover all six points.\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y= -\\tfrac13 x+ \\tfrac43 ,\\qquad \n  \\ell_2:\\;y= -\\tfrac23 x+ \\tfrac53 ,\\qquad \n  \\ell_3:\\;y= -x+4 .\n  \\]\n  All three lines have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) and each passes through two points of \\(T_3\\); together they cover the whole set.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfying the two conditions exists.  \nWe shall construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\n\\]\n\nthe new points that appear when we pass from \\(T_n\\) to \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nThe set \\(U_{n+1}\\) consists of the \\(n+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n+1),\\;(2,n),\\dots ,\\;(n+1,1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Obtaining the same number of sunny lines (\\(k\\) unchanged)\n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) for \\(T_n\\).  \nChoose one of the three forbidden directions that does **not** already appear among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\); such a direction always exists because \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains at most \\(k\\le n\\) lines, while there are three forbidden directions.  \n\n*If a horizontal line is free*: add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\; y = n+2 .\n\\]\n\n*If a vertical line is free*: add  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}:\\; x = n+2 .\n\\]\n\n*If a line of slope \\(-1\\) is free*: add  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d}:\\; x+y = n+3 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines passes through **all** points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) and through none of the points of \\(T_n\\); consequently it is distinct from every line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and now covers \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup U_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Increasing the number of sunny lines by one (\\(k\\to k+1\\))\n\nAgain start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nSelect a slope \\(\\alpha\\) that is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nBecause there are infinitely many real numbers, such an \\(\\alpha\\) can be chosen.\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\alpha}:\\; y = \\alpha x + \\beta ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\beta\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\in U_{n+1}\\); i.e. \\(\\beta = n+1-\\alpha\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), the line \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) meets the other points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) at distinct abscissas; in particular it also passes through the point \\((n+1,1)\\) because\n\n\\[\n1 = \\alpha (n+1) + \\beta \\iff \\beta = 1-\\alpha (n+1).\n\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(\\beta\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn+1-\\alpha = 1-\\alpha (n+1) \\Longrightarrow \\alpha = -\\frac{n}{n+2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{*}:\\; y = -\\frac{n}{n+2}\\,x + \\frac{2n+2}{n+2}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-\\dfrac{n}{n+2}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\) and passes through **both** endpoints \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\) of the set \\(U_{n+1}\\); consequently it contains every intermediate point of \\(U_{n+1}\\) as well (the points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) lie on a straight line of slope \\(-1\\), and \\(\\ell^{*}\\) coincides with that line only when its slope is \\(-1\\), which it is not). Hence \\(\\ell^{*}\\) covers the whole new layer \\(U_{n+1}\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell^{*}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe added line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is sunny and distinct from all previous lines (its slope is new), so the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k+1\\). The family now has \\(n+1\\) lines and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. The extreme case \\(k=n+1\\)\n\nApply the construction of \u00a72 repeatedly, starting from the configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k=n\\). Each step adds one sunny line and removes the need for a forbidden line, eventually yielding a family of \\(n+1\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe two procedures above show that, assuming the statement true for \\(n\\), we can obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\) with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1\\). Since the base case \\(n=3\\) has been verified, the statement holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus **all** non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are admissible values of \\(k\\).  (The case \\(k=n+1\\) is impossible because at most \\(n\\) lines are allowed.)"
