ready appear in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\); such a direction always exists because at most three forbidden directions exist while \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains at most \\(n\\) lines.  \n       Add a line of that direction passing through all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) (e.g. \\(y=n+2\\), \\(x=n+2\\) or \\(x+y=n+3\\)).  \n       The new line is distinct from the previous ones, does not increase the number of sunny lines, and together with \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n       Hence we obtain \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n   * (ii) **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one** (\\(k\\to k+1\\)).  \n       Start again from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n       Pick a slope \\(\\sigma\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the already‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (possible because there are infinitely many real numbers).  \n       Consider the line  \n\n       \\[\n       \\ell_{\\sigma}:\\; y=\\sigma x + b,\n       \\]\n\n       where \\(b\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\) passes through the first point of \\(U_{n+1}\\), namely \\((1,n+1)\\); thus \\(b=n+1-\\sigma\\).  \n       This line is sunny and distinct from all previous lines.  \n       It covers the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and, because its slope is not \\(-1\\), it meets the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (which contains all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\)) in exactly one point, namely \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n       Consequently the remaining \\(n\\) points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) are still uncovered.  \n       To cover them we add **one** forbidden line, chosen among the three directions that is still unused in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n       For instance, if the horizontal direction is free we add the line \\(y=n+2\\); this line contains **all** points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) and therefore also the points not covered by \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\).  \n       Because the horizontal line was not present before, it is distinct from the existing lines.  \n\n       The resulting family  \n\n       \\[\n       \\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\sigma},\\; \\text{chosen forbidden line}\\}\n       \\]\n\n       has \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k+1\\) of which are sunny, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n   By repeatedly applying (i) and (ii) we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   Since the base case supplies the configurations for \\(n=3\\), the induction proves the claim for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the constructive proof that every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\subset\\text{lines},\\;|\\mathcal L|=n,\n      \\;T_n\\subset\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L,\\;\n      \\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ sunny}\\}=k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThe inclusion \\(K_n\\subseteq\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is immediate because a family contains at most \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThe rest of the proof is devoted to showing that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) belongs to \\(K_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.  The following four families of three distinct lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\)** (no sunny line)  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=1\\)**  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n  L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=2\\)**  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43 ,\\qquad \n  L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac23x+\\tfrac53 .\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\)**  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43 ,\\qquad \n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac23x+\\tfrac53 ,\\qquad \n  L_3:\\;y=-x+4 .\n  \\]\n\nIn each family the three lines are distinct, they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\), and the indicated number of them are sunny.  Hence \\(K_3=\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfying the two required properties for the set \\(T_n\\).  \nWe shall construct families \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) for all \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\n      =\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup U_{n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged  \n\nLet \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nAmong the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\text{horizontal }(slope\\;0),\\;\n            \\text{vertical }(slope\\;\\infty),\\;\n            \\text{diagonal }(slope\\;-1)\\}\n\\]\n\nchoose one, say \\(d\\), that does **not** appear as the direction of any line in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nSuch a direction exists because at most three directions are forbidden while \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains at most \\(n\\) lines.\n\nAdd the line of direction \\(d\\) that passes through all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\):\n\n* if \\(d\\) is horizontal, add \\(y=n+2\\);\n* if \\(d\\) is vertical, add \\(x=n+2\\);\n* if \\(d\\) is diagonal, add \\(x+y=n+3\\).\n\nCall the added line \\(L_{new}\\).  \n\\(L_{new}\\) is distinct from the previous lines (it contains a point of \\(U_{n+1}\\) which none of the old lines contains) and it is **not** sunny.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n\\) we have produced a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nNow let \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and start again from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\sigma\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\sigma\\neq\\text{slope}(L)\\ \\text{for every sunny }L\\in\\mathcal L_{n,k}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the set of forbidden slopes is finite, such a \\(\\sigma\\) exists.\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\sigma}:\\; y=\\sigma x + b,\\qquad b=n+1-\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nso that \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\) passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\in U_{n+1}\\).  \n\\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\) is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (its slope is new) and is sunny.\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\) covers the single point \\((1,n+1)\\) of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\neq -1\\), \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\) meets that diagonal in exactly one point, namely \\((1,n+1)\\); therefore the other \\(n\\) points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) are still uncovered.\n\nTo cover them we add **one** forbidden line, again choosing a direction from \\(\\mathcal D\\) that is not already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nFor definiteness, suppose the horizontal direction is free; we add the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{h}:\\; y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{h}\\) contains **all** points of \\(U_{n+1}\\), in particular the \\(n\\) points not covered by \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\).  \nBecause the horizontal direction was absent before, \\(L_{h}\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and from \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{\\sigma},L_{h}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family has \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k+1\\) of which are sunny (the former sunny lines plus \\(\\ell_{\\sigma}\\)), and it covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nIf the horizontal direction is already used, we use instead the vertical or diagonal direction; the argument is identical.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n\\) we have produced a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with **one more** sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  Completion of the induction  \n\nStarting from the base families for \\(n=3\\) we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) by repeatedly applying the two constructions:\n\n* to keep \\(k\\) unchanged while increasing \\(n\\) (construction 3.1);\n* to increase \\(k\\) by one while increasing \\(n\\) (construction 3.2).\n\nConsequently for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfying the problem’s requirements.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because at most \\(n\\) lines are available.  Hence the problem is completely solved."
