 points belongs to \\(T_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad c+d\\le n+1,\\qquad e+f\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) to replace \\(c\\) by \\(b\\) and \\(d\\) by \\(a\\) in the second inequality of\n(4) yields again \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The third inequality together with \\(e+f=b\\)\ngives \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Combining this with the first inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\nforces \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\).  But every\npoint of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\), a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Corollary 4.**  \nEach of the three directions can be used at most once; therefore the total\nnumber \\(s\\) of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\ns\\le 2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessary lower bound for sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.  Since \\(k=n-s\\) and (5) gives \\(s\\le2\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\nConsequently the only possible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions showing sufficiency  \n\nWe now exhibit, for each value in (7), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly that many sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.1  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -x + (n+1-i). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\) shifted by a constant; therefore it is **not**\nparallel to the forbidden directions and is sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb = -a + (a+b) \\le -a + (n+1).\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,n+1-(a+b)}\\), whose index is an integer\nbetween \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  This gives a\nconfiguration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.2  The case \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line)\n\nKeep the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) from (8) and replace \\(L_0\\)\nby the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) is non\u2011sunny (vertical).  The remaining sunny lines are unchanged, so we have\n\\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(V\\).  If \\(a\\ge2\\) then\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and consequently the point lies on the\nsunny line \\(L_{\\,n+1-(a+b)}\\) (which is among \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) because\n\\(a+b\\ge3\\)).  Hence all points are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.3  The case \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nTake the vertical line \\(V:x=1\\) and the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  For the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines we use the sunny family  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -x + (n+1-i),\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct sunny lines (their intercepts are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,2\\)).\n\nNow consider any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b\\ge4\\), so\n  \\(n+1-(a+b)\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n-2\\).  Consequently the point\n  lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,n+1-(a+b)}\\) from (9).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly two lines are non\u2011sunny,\nso \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-2\\) and exhibited explicit\nconstructions for \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of\nsunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
