H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, all lines are distinct, and  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq \\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} L .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny by construction and distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \nEach \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the point \\(P_{i}\\in S\\).  \n\nConsider an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le k\\):*  \nThen \\(b\\) equals some index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nThe line \\(L_{i}\\) has equation \\(y=1+m_{i}(x-i)\\).  \nSetting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1+m_{i}(a-i)\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\); the right‑hand side equals \\(b\\) precisely when \\(a\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb-1=m_{i}(a-i)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;a=i+\\frac{b-1}{m_{i}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m_{i}\\) is a non‑zero real number, the expression on the right is a real number; the equality holds for the given integer \\(a\\) because \\((a,b)\\) is a point of the set we are testing.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n*If \\(b>k\\):*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) (which is still present in \\(\\mathcal L\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\) by definition of \\(H_{b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 shows that the \\(n\\) horizontal lines alone already satisfy the covering requirement (with \\(k=0\\)).  \nLemma 2 shows how, for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we can replace exactly \\(k\\) of those horizontal lines by distinct sunny lines while preserving the covering property and keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nNo 