}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Group C – turning \\(k\\) of the diagonal lines into sunny lines.*  \n\n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   4\\le c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each chosen \\(c_i\\) we replace the diagonal line \\(d_{c_i}\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that still passes through **one** point of the set  \n   \\[\n   D_{c_i}=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c_i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Construction of \\(\\sigma_i\\): pick the point \\((c_i-1,1)\\in D_{c_i}\\) (its second coordinate equals \\(1\\)).  \n   Choose a slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-(c_i-1)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\((c_i-1,1)\\), and is distinct from all previously defined lines because its slope differs from the slopes of \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\) (which are \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\)) and from the slopes of the remaining diagonal lines (all equal \\(-1\\)).\n\n   *Why the whole set \\(S_n\\) remains covered.*\n\n   - Points of \\(B_n\\) are covered either by \\(\\ell_h\\) (if their abscissa is not among the chosen \\(\\{c_i-1\\}\\)) or by the appropriate sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) (if the point is \\((c_i-1,1)\\)).\n   - Points of \\(V_n\\) are all covered by \\(\\ell_v\\).\n   - For a sum \\(c\\) **not** selected (i.e. \\(c\\neq c_i\\) for all \\(i\\)), every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(d_c\\), which is present in the family.\n   - For a selected sum \\(c_i\\), the point \\((c_i-1,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n     Every other point of the same diagonal has both coordinates at least \\(2\\); such a point also belongs to the diagonal line \\(d_{c_i+1}\\) **unless** \\(c_i=n+1\\).  \n     When \\(c_i=n+1\\) the only interior point of that diagonal is \\((n,1)\\), which is precisely the point \\((c_i-1,1)\\) already covered by \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n     Hence all interior points of a replaced diagonal are covered by a diagonal line that remains in the family (either \\(d_{c_i+1}\\) or, for the maximal diagonal, by the sunny line itself).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n   *Counting the lines.*\n\n   - Two fixed non‑sunny lines: \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\);\n   - \\((n-2)-k\\) unchanged diagonal lines \\(d_c\\);\n   - \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\n   The total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   2+\\bigl((n-2)-k\\bigr)+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that any admissible family must contain at least one horizontal and one vertical line, i.e. at least two non‑sunny lines. Hence the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n-2\\). In particular the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   For each such \\(k\\) an explicit construction has been given, and the impossibility of larger \\(k\\) follows from the necessity of at least two non‑sunny lines.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
