each replacement, the process can be continued until exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines have been introduced, provided that at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain (hence the restriction \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\n\nThe constructions are completely explicit; all coordinates are given in closed form, and the verification that the covering condition holds is a straightforward check.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y = i \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor),\\\\[2mm]\nM_j &: x = j \\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1),\\\\[2mm]\nD   &: x+y = n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(L_i\\) are horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), all the \\(M_j\\) are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+\\bigl(\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\bigr)+1=n .\n\\]\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have either \\(b\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) or \\(a\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_b\\).  \n\n*If \\(b> \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(M_a\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  (Introducing sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nStart from the configuration of Lemma\u202f1, which contains \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nWe shall replace, one by one, some of these non\u2011sunny lines by sunny lines while keeping the covering property.  \n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), after we have introduced \\(k\\) sunny lines there will still be at least two non\u2011sunny lines left; this fact will be used to guarantee that the replacement process can be continued.\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Choice of a point to be newly covered\n\nAssume that after \\(t\\) replacements (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSelect a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F_t\\) that is **horizontal** (the case of a vertical line is analogous).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line \\(y = h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(h\\) are already covered by \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf we delete \\(\\ell\\) from the family, the points that become uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+h\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(|U_h| = n+1-h\\ge 2\\) because \\(h\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Adding a sunny line through the uncovered points\n\nPick two distinct points of \\(U_h\\), say  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,h),\\qquad P_2=(n+1-h,h).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{h-h}{(n+1-h)-1}=0,\n\\]\n\nso it would be horizontal again, which is not allowed.  \nInstead we choose a third point of \\(U_h\\) that is not collinear with \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nP_3=(2,h-1)\\in T_n\\quad\\text{(this point exists because }h\\ge 2\\text{ when }h>1\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) are not collinear; the line \\(\\ell'\\) determined by \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm'=\\frac{h-(h-1)}{1-2}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden.  \nHence we use the line through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\). Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm''=\\frac{h-(h-1)}{(n+1-h)-2}= \\frac{1}{n-1-h}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le h\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(n-1-h\\ge \\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(m''\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThus the line \\(\\ell''\\) through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) is sunny.\n\nMoreover \\(\\ell''\\) contains the two points \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) of \\(U_h\\); the remaining points of \\(U_h\\) are still covered by the vertical lines \\(x=j\\) that belong to the original family (these vertical lines have not been removed). Hence after deleting the horizontal line \\(\\ell\\) and inserting the sunny line \\(\\ell''\\) we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\) of \\(n\\) lines, now containing \\(t+1\\) sunny lines, and still covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Iteration\n\nRepeating the above replacement \\(k\\) times we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nThe construction works as long as after each step at least one horizontal (or vertical) non\u2011sunny line remains to be removed; this is guaranteed because we stop after \\(k\\le n-2\\) steps, leaving at least two non\u2011sunny lines untouched. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the admissible value \\(k=0\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we obtain that every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nis also admissible.  \nThus all 