ny lines remain (hence the restriction \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\n\nAll coordinates are given explicitly, and the verification that the covering condition holds is a straightforward check.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_i &: y = i \\qquad &&\\bigl(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nV_j &: x = j \\qquad &&\\bigl(j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nD   &: x+y = n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(H_i\\) are horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), all the \\(V_j\\) are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+\\bigl(\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\bigr)+1=n .\n\\]\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have either \\(b\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) or \\(a\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b> \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  (Introducing sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nStart from the configuration of Lemma 1, which contains \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nWe shall replace, one by one, some of these non‑sunny lines by sunny lines while keeping the covering property.  \n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), after we have introduced \\(k\\) sunny lines there will still be at least two non‑sunny lines left; this fact guarantees that at each step we can find a non‑sunny line whose removal does not destroy the ability to cover the points that become uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume that after \\(t\\) replacements (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) we have a family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nChoose a horizontal line \\(H\\in\\mathcal F_t\\); such a line exists because at the beginning there are \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge 1\\) horizontal lines and we never delete all of them (we stop before fewer than two non‑sunny lines remain).\n\nWrite \\(H\\) as \\(y=h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(h\\) are covered by \\(H\\).  \nIf we delete \\(H\\) from the family, the set of points that become uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nU_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+h\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nNote that \\(|U_h|=n+1-h\\ge 2\\) because \\(h\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction of a sunny replacement\n\nSelect the two extreme points of \\(U_h\\),\n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,h),\\qquad P_2=(n+1-h,h).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) is horizontal, hence not suitable.  \nChoose a third point of \\(U_h\\) that is not collinear with \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2