"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved the following two facts, which together determine the set of possible \\(k\\) completely:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   - Show that among any \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   - The argument uses the pigeon\u2011hole principle on the set of points \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) which lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\). Because a sunny line cannot contain two of these points (its slope would have to be \\(-1\\)), each of these \\(n\\) points must be covered by a distinct line, forcing at least two of the covering lines to be non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   - Start with the \u201cstandard\u201d covering consisting of the \\(n\\) lines  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = -x + (i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n,\n     \\]\n     which are all non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   - Replace, one by one, the first \\(k\\) of these lines by sunny lines that still cover the required points.  \n   - For each \\(i\\le k\\) replace \\(L_i\\) by the sunny line  \n     \\[\n     S_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + 1,\n     \\]\n     which passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i+1,i)\\).  \n   - Verify that after each replacement the set of lines still covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). This is done by observing that the points with \\(a\\le i\\) are now covered by the new sunny line \\(S_i\\), while the points with \\(a>i\\) remain covered by the untouched non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_{i+1},\\dots ,L_n\\).  \n   - Because each replacement introduces exactly one sunny line and does not affect the covering property, after \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   - Suppose, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Then only one line would be non\u2011sunny, say parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   - The set of points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, none of which can lie on a sunny line (a sunny line cannot have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Hence all these \\(n\\) points would have to lie on the single non\u2011sunny line, which is impossible because they are not collinear unless the line is \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is forbidden (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \n   - The same contradiction arises if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, because then the \\(n\\) points on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) would have nowhere to go.  \n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them belong to the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nIf a line is sunny, its slope cannot be \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set \\(\\{P_i\\}\\).  \n\nSince the \\(n\\) points in (1) must be covered by the \\(n\\) given lines, at least \\(n-(\\text{number of sunny lines})\\) of the lines are forced to contain a point of \\(\\{P_i\\}\\).  \nIf there were \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines, then at most one (respectively none) of the lines could contain a point of \\(\\{P_i\\}\\), which is impossible because the \\(n\\) distinct points need \\(n\\) distinct lines to be covered.  \n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A family of coverings with any prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-x+(i+1) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore all \\(L_i\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nThe collection \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\): indeed a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le -a+(n+1)\\); the integer \\(i:=n+1-b-a\\) lies in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), and then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i+1}\\).\n\nNow fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) replace the line \\(L_i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(S_i\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is also finite, so the line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. Hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification that the new family still covers \\(T_n\\).*  \nFix \\(i\\le k\\). The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\qquad (i+1,i) .\n\\]\n\nConsequently it contains every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le i+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le i+2\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a\\le i\\) belong to the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le i+1\\}\\) and are therefore covered by \\(S_i\\).  \n\nFor points with \\(a\\ge i+1\\) we still have the untouched lines \\(L_{i+1},L_{i+2},\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nIndeed, if \\(a\\ge i+1\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le -a+(n+1)\\le - (i+1)+(n+1)=n-i\\); hence the point lies on \\(L_{a-i}\\), which is among the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus after the replacement of the first \\(k\\) lines we obtain a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k, L_{k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non\u2011sunny; it must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nAll points \\(P_i\\) defined in (1) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\).  \nSince a sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\), none of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can contain any \\(P_i\\).  \nConsequently all \\(n\\) points \\(P_i\\) would have to lie on the single non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) were horizontal or vertical, the points \\(P_i\\) are not collinear, a contradiction.  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) were parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), its equation would be \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\); the only value of \\(c\\) that contains any of the \\(P_i\\) is \\(c=n+1\\), but the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself is forbidden because its slope is \\(-1\\).  \nHence a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the same argument shows that the \\(n\\) points \\(P_i\\) would have nowhere to be placed, which is impossible.  \n\nTherefore no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nSection\u202f2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSection\u202f3 shows that the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
