x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)*: keep the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and add the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (which passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)).  \n   *\\(k=2\\)*: keep the \\(n-2\\) diagonals \\(x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and add the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) (through \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) and \\(y=x+1\\) (through \\((1,2),(2,3)\\)).  \n   *\\(k=3\\)*: keep the \\(n-3\\) diagonals \\(x+y=5,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and add the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n   \\ell_2:\\;y=x+1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_3:\\;y=x+2,\n   \\]\n\n   which respectively cover the pairs \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\{(1,2),(2,3)\\},\\{(1,3),(2,4)\\}\\).  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines alone cover all six points, so the construction still works.\n\n   In each case the \\(n\\) lines are distinct and together they contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN:=|\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) then those three points are collinear.  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) have integer coordinates, so the line\u2019s slope is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nIf the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), the only way three lattice points with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates can be collinear is that the slope equals \\(-1\\) (the points would then lie on a line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nThus a sunny line (slope \\(\\neq -1\\)) cannot contain three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\). \u220e\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in a configuration. By Lemma\u202f1 they cover at most \\(2s\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Capacity of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith integer \\(c\\) satisfying \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\n* For \\(x=c\\) we have the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence the line contains \\(n+1-c\\) points.\n* For \\(y=c\\) the count is the same, \\(n+1-c\\).\n* For \\(x+y=c\\) we have the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); thus the line contains \\(c-1\\) points.\n\nConsequently the capacities of the non\u2011sunny lines are the integers  \n\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,n\\quad\\text{(for the diagonals)}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nn-1,n-2,\\dots ,1\\quad\\text{(for vertical/horizontal lines)}.\n\\]\n\nTo maximise the total number of points that can be covered by a given number \\(t\\) of non\u2011sunny lines we simply choose the \\(t\\) lines with the largest capacities, i.e. the diagonals \\(x+y=n+1,\\,x+y=n,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-t\\). Their total capacity is\n\n\\[\nC(t)=\\sum_{i=n+2-t}^{n+1}(i-1)\n      =\\frac{(n+t+1)(t)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Deriving the inequality for \\(s\\)\n\nIn a configuration with \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n2s + C(n-s)=2s+\\frac{(n+s+1)(n-s)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  \nSince all \\(N=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, we require\n\n\\[\n2s+\\frac{(n+s+1)(n-s)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (1) by \\(2\\) and expanding the product gives  \n\n\\[\n4s + n^{2}-s^{2}+n-s \\ge n^{2}+n,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n-s^{2}+3s\\ge0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad s(3-s)\\ge0.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le 3.\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\); together they cover \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\). All are non\u2011sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are covered by the \\(n-1\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=3,\\,x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. by the lines \\(L_i\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThus we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=2\\)\n\nAdd the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=x+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich cover the point pairs \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,2),(2,3)\\}\\) respectively.  \nThe remaining points are covered by the \\(n-2\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=4,\\,x+y=5,\\dots ,x+y=n+1.\n\\]\n\nHence we obtain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### (iv)  \\(k=3\\)\n\nTake the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=x+1,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=x+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich together contain the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,2),\\;(1,2),(2,3),\\;(1,3),(2,4).\n\\]\n\nThe other points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) are covered by the \\(n-3\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=5,\\,x+y=6,\\dots ,x+y=n+1.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) alone already covers all six points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_3\\); for larger \\(n\\) the additional diagonals are needed. In every case we have exactly three sunny lines.\n\nAll the lines in each construction are distinct, and each construction satisfies the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe inequality derived in Section\u202f3 shows that a configuration can contain at most three sunny lines, and the explicit constructions of Section\u202f4 demonstrate that \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,3.}\n\\]"
