1,n-1)\\) would then require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct non\u2011parallel lines, contradicting the hypothesis.  Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_i &: y=i \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2mm]\nV   &: x=1,\\\\[2mm]\nD   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(H_i\\) are horizontal, \\(V\\) is vertical and \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is \\((n-2)+1+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-(n-1)=2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines.  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(h\\) with \\(1\\le h\\le n-2\\).  \nConsider the horizontal line \\(H_h\\colon y=h\\).  Removing \\(H_h\\) would leave uncovered the set  \n\n\\[\nU_h=\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,h)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(V\\); the point \\((n+1-h,h)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-h)+