 cannot be positive; the only possible value is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_i &: y=i \\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2mm]\nV   &: x=1,\\\\[2mm]\nD   &: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll the \\(H_i\\) are horizontal, \\(V\\) is vertical and \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is \\((n-2)+1+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-(n-1)=2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines.  \nHence a configuration with **no** sunny lines always exists; therefore \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma: a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one point\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) and \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause both points belong to \\(T_n\\), we have \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) for \\(i=1,2\\).  \nIf \\(a_1=a_2\\) the line would be vertical, 