            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is obtained by summing the number of admissible \\(b\\) for each \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\sum_{s=1}^{n-1}s=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A sunny line cannot contain many points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) we have \\(b=ma+c\\) and the constraints  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n-1,\\qquad 1\\le ma+c\\le n-a .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities in (3) are linear in \\(a\\); consequently the set of admissible integer \\(a\\) is an interval of integers.  \nBecause the slope \\(m\\neq 0\\), the interval length is at most the length of the projection of the triangle onto the \\(x\\)-axis after being sheared by the factor \\(|m|\\). A direct computation shows that the maximal possible number of integer \\(a\\) satisfying (3) is \\(n-2\\) (attained, for example, when \\(m=1\\) and the line passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,n-2)\\)). Hence (2) holds. ∎\n\n\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\): a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\)) meets the triangle in the segment \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-b\\}\\), which has \\(n-b\\le n-1\\) lattice points; the same bound holds for vertical and diagonal lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(s=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nUsing Lemma 2 and the observation above we obtain the following estimate for the total number of points that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\le k\\,(n-2)+s\\,(n-1)\n   =k\\,(n-2)+(n-k)(n-1)\n   =n(n-1)-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert the exact value (1) of \\(|T_n|\\) into (4):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n(n-1)-k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\); we shall build a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Lines that will always be present (non‑sunny):*  \n\n\\[\nL_V:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad\nL_D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines that will be kept:* for each integer \\(h\\) with \\(t+1\\le h\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=h .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\((n-2)-t\\) such horizontal lines.\n\n*Sunny lines that replace the first \\(t\\) horizontals:* for each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (1-i) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\nt\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2-t)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{non‑sunny}) = n\n\\]\n\nlines, exactly \\(k=t\\) of which are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Three mutually exclusive cases occur.\n\n1. **\\(b\\le t\\).**  \n   Then \\(b = a + (1-b)\\) gives \\(b = a + (1-b)\\) ⇔ \\(a = b\\).  \n   Since \\(b\\le t\\), the point \\((a,b)=(b,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\) (because \\(S_b\\) has equation \\(y=x+(1-b)\\) and substituting \\(x=b\\) yields \\(y=b\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n2. **\\(t+1\\le b\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The horizontal line \\(L_b\\) (which is present) contains all points with second coordinate \\(b\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).\n\n3. **\\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).**  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\) when \\(b=n\\) and \\(a\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) when \\(b=n-1\\).  \n   - If \\(a=1\\) we are on the vertical line \\(L_V\\).  \n   - If \\((a,b)=(2,n-1)\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(L_D\\).\n\nAll possibilities are exhausted, therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\nThe lines are distinct: the two non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); the remaining horizontals have slope \\(0\\); the sunny lines all have slope \\(1\\) but distinct intercepts, so no two coincide.\n\nThus for each \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we have produced a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines and covering all required points.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (5) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, while the construction of § 4 proves that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}}.\n\\]  \n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
