quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = b+r +1-b \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad r = 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the only points with \\(b\\le t\\) that are not on a horizontal line are those with \\(a=b\\); these are precisely the points \\((i,i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le t\\), and each such point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) (indeed \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((i,i)\\) because \\(i = i + (1-i)\\)). Thus every point with \\(b\\le t\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(t+1\\le b\\le n-2\\).*  \n   The horizontal line \\(L_{b}\\) (which is present) contains all points whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).*  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\((a,b)=(1,n)\\) or \\((a,b)=(1,n-1)\\) or \\((2,n-1)\\).  \n   - \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{V}\\).  \n   - \\((1,n-1)\\) also lies on \\(L_{V}\\).  \n   - \\((2,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{D}\\).\n\n   All possibilities are exhausted, so every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n5. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The two non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). All remaining horizontals have slope \\(0\\). The sunny lines all have slope \\(1\\) but distinct intercepts \\(1-i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\)), hence they are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the horizontals. Consequently the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
