(Indeed, solving \\(b = a + (b-1)\\) gives \\(a=1\\); for \\(a>1\\) the point lies on the same line but farther to the right, still inside the triangle because the line \\(S_{b}\\) enters the triangle at \\((1,b)\\) and exits at \\((n+1-b,b+n-1)\\), and all intermediate integer points are present.)\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(k+1\\le b\\le n-2\\).*  \n   The horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) (which is present) contains all points whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f3: \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).*  \n   The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\) when \\(b=n\\) and forces \\(a\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) when \\(b=n-1\\).  \n   - If \\((a,b)=(1,n)\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(L_{V}\\).  \n   - If \\((a,b)=(1,n-1)\\) it also lies on \\(L_{V}\\).  \n   - If \\((a,b)=(2,n-1)\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{D}\\).\n\n   All possibilities are covered, completing the verification.\n\n5. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The two non\u2011sunny lines have slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). All horizontals have slope \\(0\\). The sunny lines all have slope \\(1\\) but distinct intercepts \\(i-1\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)). Hence no two lines coincide, and we indeed have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit construction for every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
