            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_1=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_2=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf d_3=(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary\n\nDefine the three “border’’ subsets of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_x&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nB_y&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nB_d&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B_x\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), \\(B_y\\) on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and \\(B_d\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(B_x,B_y,B_d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(B_x\\) they would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, contradicting sunniness. The same argument works for \\(B_y\\) (horizontal) and for \\(B_d\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently, if a configuration uses \\(s\\) sunny lines, the total number of border points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(3s\\).\n\nNow count the border points.  \n\\(|B_x|=n,\\;|B_y|=n,\\;|B_d|=n-1\\).  \nThe three vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\) belong to two of the sets each, so after correcting the double count we obtain\n\n\\[\n|B_x\\cup B_y\\cup B_d| = n+n+(n-1)-2 = 3n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny. Then the number of sunny lines is at least \\(n-1\\). By Lemma 1 the sunny lines can cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) border points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n3n-2 \\le 3(n-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(-2\\le -3\\), an impossibility. Hence **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Denoting by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, whose union contains \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Two non‑sunny lines\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny and together they cover the whole sets \\(B_y\\) and \\(B_x\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Interior points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I_n = \\mathcal T_n\\setminus (B_x\\cup B_y) \n            = \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are the interior lattice points of the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\). Their cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I_n| = \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines through interior points\n\nFor any interior point \\((a,b)\\) define the line\n\n\\[\nS_{a,b}:\\; y-2 = \\frac{b-2}{a-1}\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\), the slope \\(\\frac{b-2}{a-1}\\) is finite and non‑zero. Moreover, if the slope were \\(-1\\) we would have \\(b-2 = -(a-1)\\), i.e. \\(a+b=1\\), impossible for interior points. Hence each \\(S_{a,b}\\) is sunny. Distinct interior points give distinct slopes, therefore the lines \\(S_{a,b}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 2.4 Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n- **Case \\(k=n-2\\).**  \n  Choose any \\(n-2\\) interior points, for instance all points with \\(a+b=n\\). The corresponding \\(n-2\\) sunny lines together with \\(L_1,L_2\\) give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k=n-2\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on one of them (the chosen interior points lie on their own sunny line, all other interior points are covered because each lies on some line \\(S_{a,b}\\) with the same slope as the line through the chosen point having the same sum \\(a+b\\); alternatively, we may simply keep *all* interior points each on its own sunny line, which yields at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and then discard any surplus sunny lines by merging, as described below).\n\n- **Case \\(k<n-2\\).**  \n  Start from the arrangement that uses the \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\{S_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal I_n\\}\\).  \n  Pick two interior points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\). The line through them has slope  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\n  \\]\n\n  which cannot be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because the two points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates, and different sums \\(a+b\\). Hence this line is sunny. Replace the two former sunny lines by this single line; the total number of lines stays the same (we still have \\(n\\) lines because we have removed one line but added a new one), while the number of sunny lines decreases by one. Repeating this operation \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Throughout the process all lines remain distinct: each newly created line passes through a pair of interior points that were not collinear with any previously used line.\n\n