 the line \\(L_{d}\\) (because for such points we have \\(a+b=n+1\\) after a simple reduction argument).  \n   Hence the three non‑sunny lines already satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n2. **Adding sunny lines while preserving the covering.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{t}\\colon y=\\frac{t}{t-1}\\,x-\\frac{1}{t-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{t}{t-1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover \\(S_{t}\\) passes through the two lattice points \\((t-1,1)\\) and \\((1,t-1)\\).  \n   These two points are already covered by \\(L_{h}\\) and \\(L_{v}\\), so adding \\(S_{t}\\) does not destroy the covering property.  \n   The lines \\(\\{S_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\) are pairwise distinct, giving exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Starting from the three non‑sunny lines we may now choose any subset of the sunny lines \\(S_{t}\\).  \n   If we select \\(k\\) of them (with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)) and keep the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) slots empty, we obtain a family of  \n\n   \\[\n   3+k\\le n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines that still covers all required points.  \n   To reach exactly \\(n\\) lines we fill the remaining slots with additional non‑sunny lines of the three forbidden directions (e.g. horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\) or vertical lines \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\)).  \n   These extra non‑sunny lines are distinct from the three base lines and from each other, and they do not introduce any new uncovered points, because every point of the lattice is already covered.\n\n   Consequently, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we can construct a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\nThe cases \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) remain open; the present method cannot produce enough sunny lines without losing the covering property, and a different approach would be required.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-3\\text{ are attainable (partial result).}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
