or all positive integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is precisely the requirement that the family of lines covers the set \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le c\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_c\\) are distinct because they have different constant terms. Moreover each \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny (a sunny line must avoid being parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nNow take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Set \\(c=a+b\\); then \\(c\\) is an integer satisfying \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\). Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into the equation of \\(L_c\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+b=c,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_c\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the collection \\(\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\nThe collection contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\{c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}| = n\n\\]\n\nlines, satisfying the requirement that there be \\(n\\) distinct lines. Since none of these lines is sunny, the number of sunny lines in this configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) can be achieved.\n\nNo further values of \\(k\\) have been proved possible or impossible; the determination of the full set of admissible \\(k\\) remains an open problem."

