 at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be **non‑sunny** and parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\nA symmetric argument applied to the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{1}: \\; x=1\n\\]\n\n(which contains the points \\((1,1), (1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)) shows that at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny and parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n\nThus any admissible family contains **at least two** non‑sunny lines. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction achieving every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\); we shall build a family \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the two lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad L_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (parallel respectively to the \\(y\\)-axis and to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n*Additional non‑sunny lines (if needed).*  \nIf \\(t<n-2\\) we add \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}: y=j+1 ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_{j}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(t+2\\le c\\le n\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}: y = m_{c}\\,x + (c-1-m_{c}) ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(m_{c}\\) is chosen so that  \n\n* \\(m_{c}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (ensuring sunshine),  \n* all the slopes \\(\\{m_{c}\\}\\) are distinct (so the lines are distinct).\n\nBecause the line passes through \\((1,c-1)\\), it meets the diagonal \\(D_{c}:x+y=c\\) exactly at that lattice point.\n\nNow count the lines.  \nWe have the two fixed non‑sunny lines, \\(t\\) horizontal lines, and \\((n-2-t)\\) sunny lines \\(L_{c}\\); altogether\n\n\\[\n2 + t + (n-2-t)=n\n\\]\n\nlines, as required. By construction exactly the \\(n-2-t\\) lines \\(L_{c}\\) are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-2-t\\). Since \\(t\\) ranges from \\(0\\) to \\(n-2\\), the obtained values of \\(k\\) range from \\(n-2\\) down to \\(0\\). Re‑indexing \\(t:=n-2-k\\) yields a family with any prescribed \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\).\n\n*Verification of the covering property.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(b\\le t+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is one of the \\(H_{j}\\).  \n* If \\(b\\ge t+2\\) then set \\(c=a+b\\). We have \\(c\\ge t+2\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \n\n  – If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n\n  – If \\(a\\neq1\\) then \\(c\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(c=n+1\\) and the point lies on \\(L_{d}\\)). Hence \\(c\\) is one of the indices for which a sunny line \\(L_{c}\\) was introduced, and by construction \\(L_{c}\\) passes through \\((1,c-1)\\) and meets the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in exactly one point, namely \\((a,b)\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{c}\\).\n\nIn every case the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\). Hence the family satisfies the required covering condition.\n\nTherefore for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny. By Lemma 1 at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, a contradiction. The same argument rules out the case \\(k=n\\). Hence no admissible family can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility result (Section 3) with the explicit constructions (Section 2) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible