            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\nthe \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nAny family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) contains at least two non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in **at most one** point.  \nIf none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) coincides with \\(D_{n+1}\\), at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines would be needed to cover those \\(n\\) points, leaving no line to cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(D_{n+1}\\) itself must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). Since \\(D_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), it is non‑sunny.\n\nNow look at the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{1}:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect a vertical line in at most one point, so the same counting argument forces the inclusion of \\(V_{1}\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\). The line \\(V_{1}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny. ∎  \n\nThus every admissible family contains at least two non‑sunny lines, and consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2-t .\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(i) Two mandatory non‑sunny lines**\n\n\\[\nL_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal).\n\n**(ii) Additional non‑sunny lines** (horizontal)\n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}: y=j+1 .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines**\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(t+2\\le c\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y = \\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{c-1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{c}^{(1)}=(1,c-1),\\qquad P_{c}^{(2)}=(c-1,1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\dfrac{c-2}{c-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.  \nFor distinct values of \\(c\\) the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_{c}\\) are all different.\n\n**(iv) The family**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\mathcal L_t=\n\\{L_{v},L_{h}\\}\\cup\\{H_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le t\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid t+2\\le c\\le n\\}}\n\\]\n\ncontains  \n\n\\[\n2\\;+\\;t\\;+\\;(n-2-t)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which exactly the \\(n-2-t=k\\) lines \\(L_{c}\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le t+1\\).*  \nThen \\(b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{j}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\ge t+2\\).*  \nSet \\(c=a+b\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge t+2\\),\n\n\\[\nc\\ge t+3,\\qquad c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(c=n+1\\) the point belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); this diagonal is covered by the vertical line \\(L_{v}\\) (the point \\((1,n)\\)) together with the sunny line \\(L_{n}\\) (which contains all points of the diagonal with coordinates \\(\\ge 2\\)).  \n\nIf \\(c\\le n\\) then \\(c\\in\\{t+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the line \\(L_{c}\\) was introduced.  \nBoth points \\(P_{c}^{(1)}=(1,c-1)\\) and \\(P_{c}^{(2)}=(c-1,1)\\) lie on \\(L_{c}\\); the segment of \\(L_{c}\\) between them consists exactly of the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{c}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 gives the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k=n-2-t\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\). Consequently every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable, while \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
