 every admissible family satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(t\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2-t .\n\\]\n\nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**1. Two mandatory non‑sunny lines**\n\n\\[\nL_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny.\n\n**2. Additional non‑sunny horizontal lines**\n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) put  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}: y=j+1 .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n**3. Sunny lines**\n\nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(t+2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\; y=\\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{c-1}.\n\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_{c}^{(1)}=(1,c-1),\\qquad P_{c}^{(2)}=(c-1,1).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{c-1-1}{1-(c-1)}=\\frac{c-2}{2-c}= -\\frac{c-2}{c-2}= -1,\n\\]\nand solving for the equation of the line through these two points yields exactly (★).  \nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{c-2}{c-1}\\) is never \\(0\\) (that would require \\(c=2\\), which is excluded), never undefined (the denominator is non‑zero), and never \\(-1\\) (that would require \\(c-2=-(c-1)\\), i.e. \\(c= \\tfrac12\\), impossible). Hence each \\(L_{c}\\) is sunny.  \nFor distinct values of \\(c\\) the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_{c}\\) are all different.\n\n**4. The family**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\mathcal L_t=\n\\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{y=j+1\\mid 1\\le j\\le t\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid t+2\\le c\\le n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nCounting the members gives  \n\n\\[\n2\\;+\\;t\\;+\\;(n-2-t)=n .\n\\]\n\nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) is precisely the number of lines of the third type, namely \\(n-2-t=k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le t+1\\).*  \nThen \\(b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{j}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\ge t+2\\).*  \nSet \\(c=a+b\\). Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge t+2\\),\n\n\\[\nc\\ge t+3,\\qquad c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(c=n+1\\) the point belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). The two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) of this diagonal are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); every other point of the diagonal has \\(b\\ge2\\) and therefore falls under the present case with \\(c=n+1\\). Since \\(n+1\\) is not among the indices of the sunny lines, the point is covered by the vertical or horizontal line already mentioned.\n\nIf \\(c\\le n\\) then \\(c\\in\\{t+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the line \\(L_{c}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_t\\). By construction \\(L_{c}\\) passes through the two points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); the segment of \\(L_{c}\\) between them contains **all** lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{c}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 yields the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) there exists a covering family containing exactly \\(k=n-2-t\\) sunny lines. Consequently every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable, and no larger value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the