  \\[\n   0\\le \\frac{b-1}{a}\\le \\frac{n}{1}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(k+1\\) and using \\(k\\le n-2\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le (k+1)\\frac{b-1}{a}\\le (k+1)n\\le (n-1)n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the integer \\(i\\) defined in (4) satisfies \\(0\\le i\\le k\\).  \n   If \\(i=0\\) then \\(b-1<\\dfrac{1}{k+1}a\\le a\\), which forces \\(b\\le 1\\); but \\(b>n-k\\ge 2\\) in this case, a contradiction.  Therefore \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\n\n   With this \\(i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,a + 1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) from (2).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(h_j\\)’s).  Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) the required configuration exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two non‑sunny lines, which yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration can be built.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
