nts of the same diagonal,\n\\[\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid x+y=a+b,\\;(x,y)\\neq(a,b)\\},\n\\]\nare no longer covered.\n\nFor each such point \\((x,y)\\) we add **one** of the following lines:\n\n* the vertical line \\(V_x:\\;x=x\\)   (if this vertical line has not been used before), or\n* the horizontal line \\(H_y:\\;y=y\\) (otherwise).\n\nBoth \\(V_x\\) and \\(H_y\\) are non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis or the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nBecause the set \\(\\{x\\mid (x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\}\\) consists of distinct integers, the vertical (resp. horizontal) lines introduced for different points are distinct. Hence after the replacement we have added:\n\n* one sunny line \\(S_{(a,b),m}\\);\n* exactly \\((a+b-2)\\) non\u2011sunny lines (one for each of the remaining points on the diagonal).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\)\n\nStart from the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n\\) lines.  \nLet \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be any set of size \\(|T|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in T\\) we perform the replacement described in \u00a73 on the diagonal \\(D_t\\), using the point \\((t-1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) as the distinguished point.  \n\nAfter all replacements we have added\n\n\\[\nk\\text{ sunny lines }+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nTo retain exactly \\(n\\) lines we now **delete** from the original family \\(\\mathcal D\\) a collection of diagonals whose total number equals \\(\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\).  \nSuch a collection exists because the diagonals are pairwise disjoint; we simply discard any \\(\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\) of the diagonals whose indices are not in \\(T\\).  \n\nConsequently the final family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\bigr)+\\Bigl(k+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\Bigr)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines \\(S_{(t-1,1),m_t}\\) for \\(t\\in T\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Verification of the covering property  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n* If \\(t\\notin T\\) then the diagonal \\(D_t\\) has not been removed, so \\((a,b)\\) is still covered.\n* If \\(t\\in T\\) then:\n  * when \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{(t-1,1),m_t}\\);\n  * otherwise either its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\) or its \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\) is the defining coordinate of one of the vertical/horizontal lines added in \u00a73, so \\((a,b)\\) is covered as well.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\nHence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e"
