r matches two points having the same \\(a\\), the same \\(b\\) or the same sum.\n\n   The construction yields exactly \\(\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\) disjoint pairs, each pair determining a unique line. By Lemma 1 each such line is sunny, because the two points of a pair have distinct \\(a\\), distinct \\(b\\) and distinct sums.\n\n   *Step 2 – selecting the required number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.*  \n   From the \\(\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines obtained above, choose any \\(k\\) of them (with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\)). These \\(k\\) lines will cover \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – covering the remaining points with non‑sunny lines.*  \n   The points not yet covered lie on at most \\(n-k\\) distinct “coordinates’’:\n   - For each unused value of \\(a\\) among the uncovered points, draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n   - For each unused value of \\(b\\), draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n   - For each unused value of the sum \\(a+b\\), draw the line \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   Because there are exactly \\(n\\) possible values of \\(a\\) (namely \\(1,\\dots ,n\\)), of \\(b\\) (also \\(1,\\dots ,n\\)), and of the sum \\(a+b\\) (namely \\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\)), we can always select a collection of at most \\(n-k\\) such non‑sunny lines that together contain every uncovered point. One convenient systematic choice is:\n\n   - Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   - If any point remains uncovered, replace a vertical line by the appropriate horizontal or diagonal line; the total number of lines never exceeds \\(n-k\\).\n\n   Since the non‑sunny lines are allowed to be parallel to the forbidden directions, this step always succeeds.\n\n   *Step 4 – distinctness of the lines.*  \n   All lines constructed are distinct: the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct because they arise from disjoint pairs of points; the non‑sunny lines are chosen with diff