inct sunny lines.\n\n#### 2. Selecting \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose arbitrarily any \\(k\\) of the sunny lines obtained above. They cover \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n#### 3. Covering the remaining points\n\nLet \\(T=S\\setminus\\) (points already covered).  \nFor each integer \\(a\\) that appears among the points of \\(T\\) draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\); for each integer \\(b\\) that appears among the points of \\(T\\) draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); for each integer \\(c\\) that appears as a sum \\(a+b\\) among the points of \\(T\\) draw the line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence they are non‑sunny. Because there are at most \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(a\\), at most \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(b\\), and at most \\(n\\) distinct values of the sum, we can always select at most \\(n-k\\) of them that together contain every point of \\(T\\). A concrete way is:\n\n* use the vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\);  \n* if after this some point of \\(T\\) is still uncovered, replace one of the unused vertical lines by the appropriate horizontal or diagonal line that passes through that point.\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(T\\).\n\n#### 4. Counting the lines\n\nWe have produced \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, for a total of \\(n\\) lines. Every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them, fulfilling the first requirement of the problem. The second requirement is satisfied by construction because we used precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n(n+1)/4\\rfloor\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0