o the remaining \\(N-2k\\) points have to be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines.  \nA line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions can pass through any number of points that share the same \\(a\\)-coordinate, the same \\(b\\)-coordinate or the same sum \\(a+b\\); therefore there is **no** intrinsic upper bound on how many points the non‑sunny lines can cover.  \nThe only unavoidable restriction is that the sunny lines be able to cover at least half of the points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n2k\\ge N\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le\\frac{N}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Sufficiency – explicit construction\n\nAssume an integer \\(k\\) satisfies (2). We shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n#### 4.1. Producing many sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=s\\}\n    =\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPair the points of \\(D_s\\) with the points of \\(D_{s+1}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n(1,s-1)\\leftrightarrow (s,1),\\;\n(2,s-2)\\leftrightarrow (s+1,2),\\;\\dots\n\\]\n\nContinue until all points of \\(D_s\\cup D_{s+1}\\) are exhausted, then move to the next pair of consecutive diagonals.  \nThis “staggered” pairing yields exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{N}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ndisjoint pairs of points.  \n\nIn each pair the two points have different \\(a\\)-coordinates, different \\(b\\)-coordinates and different sums; by Lemma 1 the line determined by the pair is sunny.  \nMoreover, the \\(2\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\) points involved use \\(2\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\) distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates and the same number of distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates.\n\n#### 4.2. Selecting exactly \\(k\\) sunny