rdinates and the same number of distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates.\n\n#### 4.2. Selecting exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose arbitrarily any \\(k\\) of the sunny lines obtained above (possible because \\(k\\le\\lfloor N/2\\rfloor\\) by (2)).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines are distinct and together cover exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nThe \\(2k\\) covered points involve \\(2k\\) distinct \\(a\\)-values; denote the set of those values by  \n\n\\[\nA_{\\text{used}}=\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_{2k}\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3. Covering the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=S\\setminus\\bigl(\\text{points already covered}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T\\) has its \\(a\\)-coordinate belonging to  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus A_{\\text{used}},\n\\qquad |A|=n-2k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(a\\in A\\) draw the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T\\) lie on one of these vertical lines, because each such point possesses an \\(a\\)-coordinate from \\(A\\).  \nThe lines \\(L_a\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \nTheir number is \\(n-2k\\le n-k\\) (the inequality holds because \\(k\\ge0\\)).  \n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we already have \\(n\\) vertical lines and we are done.  \nIf \\(k>0\\) we currently have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-2k\\) vertical lines, i.e. \\(n-k\\) lines in total.  \nTo reach exactly \\(n\\) lines we may add any \\(k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines (for instance the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\)); these extra lines do not affect the covering property.\n\nAll constructed lines are distinct: the sunny lines are distinct because they arise from disjoint pairs, and the vertical (and any added horizontal/diagonal) lines have different defining constants.\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.