te exactly \\(|T|=k\\) diagonals and add  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\text{ sunny lines }+ \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\text{ auxiliary lines}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F|\n   =\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+\\Bigl(k+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\Bigr)\n   =n+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(t\\in T\\) we have \\(t\\ge 2\\); the only values for which \\(t-3\\neq0\\) are \\(t=2\\) (giving \\(-1\\)) and \\(t\\ge4\\) (giving \\(\\ge1\\)).  \n   To keep the total number of lines exactly \\(n\\) we choose the set \\(T\\) **so that the sum \\(\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)\\) equals zero**.  \n   This is achieved by the following simple rule:\n\n   *If \\(k=0\\)*, take \\(T=\\varnothing\\) (the basic covering).  \n   *If \\(k\\ge1\\)*, take  \n\n   \\[\n   T=\\{3,4,\\dots ,k+2\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for this choice  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)=\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}j= \\frac{k(k-1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   and we compensate the surplus by **deleting** exactly \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) of the remaining diagonals (those with the largest indices, namely \\(D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots\\)).  \n   After this deletion the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   n-\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}+k+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The deleted diagonals are not needed any more because every point that lay on them either belongs to a diagonal in \\(T\\) (and is already covered by a sunny line or an auxiliary line) or belongs to a diagonal whose index is larger than any index kept; such points are covered by the auxiliary vertical/horizontal lines introduced for the earlier diagonals, as shown below.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(t\\notin T\\) and the diagonal \\(D_t\\) has **not** been deleted.*  \n   Then \\(D_t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *Case 2 \u2013 \\(t\\in T\\).*  \n   If \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).  \n   Otherwise \\((a,b)