sses through \\((t-1,1)\\) by construction.  \n- Rational slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) guarantees the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or \\(x+y=0\\). Hence it is sunny.  \n\n*No issue.*\n\n---\n\n### 3. Allocation rule – description\n\n> “Process the elements of \\(T\\) in increasing order of the index \\(t\\). When we meet a point \\((i,t-i)\\in P_t\\) we act as follows:\n> * If the vertical line \\(V_i\\) has not been used as an auxiliary line for any earlier point, we attach \\(V_i\\) to \\((i,t-i)\\);\n> * otherwise we attach the horizontal line \\(H_{t-i}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The rule is clearly stated.  \n- It is intended to guarantee that each auxiliary line is used at most once.  \n\n*No immediate error, but the justification that this rule indeed prevents reuse is incomplete (see Gap 1).*\n\n---\n\n### 4. “Why the rule never uses a line twice.” – vertical lines\n\n> “A vertical line \\(V_i\\) can be attached only to points whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\). Such points belong to the unique diagonal \\(D_{i+(t-i)}=D_t\\); therefore once \\(V_i\\) has been used for a point of \\(P_t\\) it cannot be needed again for any later diagonal (all later diagonals have larger index, hence larger sum of coordinates).”\n\n**Verification (vertical case):**  \n- Indeed, any point with first coordinate \\(i\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y = i + y\\).  \n- If a later diagonal has larger index \\(t'>t\\), its points satisfy \\(x+y = t' > t\\).  \n- The same \\(x=i\\) could appear on a later diagonal only if the corresponding \\(y = t' - i\\) is positive, which is possible. However, the rule would *not* need to use \\(V_i\\) again because \\(V_i\\) is already used; the rule would then try to use the horizontal line \\(H_{t'-i}\\). The argument that “it cannot be needed again” is therefore not precise: the rule *does* consider using \\(V_i\\) again, but the condition “if \\(V_i\\) has not been used” prevents it. The statement that “it cannot be needed again” is a hand‑wavy way of saying the rule’s condition blocks reuse. A rigorous proof would need to show that the condition indeed blocks reuse for all later points, which is true by definition of the rule.  \n\n*Conclusion:* The vertical‑line part is essentially correct, but the explanation is informal. **Justification Gap 1** (vertical case).\n\n---\n\n### 5. “Why the rule never uses a line twice.” – horizontal lines\n\n> “Suppose a horizontal line \\(H_j\\) were used twice. Then there would be two distinct points \\((i_1,j)\\) and \\((i_2,j)\\) with \\(i_1\\neq i_2\\) that both required \\(H_j\\). By the rule this can happen only if the corresponding vertical lines \\(V_{i_1}\\) and \\(V_{i_2}\\) had already been used earlier. But the vertical lines are used in the order of increasing diagonal index, so the first of the two points would have appeared on a diagonal with smaller index, and the second point would belong to a diagonal with larger index. When the second point is processed, its vertical line \\(V_{i_2}\\) is still unused (it has not appeared before), contradicting the premise that we were forced to use \\(H_j\\).”\n\n**Verification (horizontal case):**  \n- The argument attempts to show that a horizontal line cannot be forced to be used twice because the vertical line for the later point would still be free.  \n- However, the reasoning assumes that the vertical line \\(V_{i_2}\\) is *still* unused at the moment the second point is processed. This is not guaranteed: \\(V_{i_2}\\) could have been used earlier for a point belonging to a *different* diagonal (perhaps a diagonal not in \\(T\\) but processed earlier because of the ordering of \\(T\\)). The rule only guarantees that a vertical line is used at most once **within the processing of the set \\(T\\)**, but vertical lines could have been used for points of earlier diagonals *outside* \\(T\\) (the construction never creates auxiliary lines for diagonals not in \\(T\\)).