ting total lines after replacement\n\n> \u201cHence  \n> \\[\n> |\\mathcal F|\n> =\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+\\Bigl(k+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\Bigr)\n> =n+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The algebraic manipulation is correct: start with \\(n\\) original diagonals, delete \\(k\\) of them, add \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(\\sum(t-2)\\) auxiliary lines.  \n- The resulting expression is accurate **provided** the previous counting of auxiliary lines is correct.  \n\n*No new issue, but it depends on Gap\u202f3.*\n\n---\n\n### 8. Requirement \u201csum =\u202f0\u201d and choice of \\(T\\)\n\n> \u201cTo keep the total number of lines exactly\u202fn\u202fwe choose the set\u202fT\u202fso that the sum\u202f\u2211_{t\u2208T}(t\u20113)\u202fequals zero.  \n> If\u202fk\u22651, take\u202f\\(T=\\{3,4,\\dots ,k+2\\}\\). Indeed, for this choice  \n> \\[\n> \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)=\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}j= \\frac{k(k-1)}{2},\n> \\]\n> and we compensate the surplus by **deleting** exactly \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) of the remaining diagonals (those with the largest indices, namely \\(D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots\\)).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The claim that \u201csum =\u202f0\u201d is necessary is correct: we need \\(|\\mathcal F|=n\\).  \n- However, the construction sets the sum to \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) (positive for \\(k\\ge2\\)) and then *deletes* the same number of diagonals. The solution does **not** prove that there are always enough diagonals left to delete (i.e., that \\(n-k\\ge \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) for all \\(k\\le n\\)). For large \\(k\\) this inequality fails. For example, take \\(n=5\\) and \\(k=5\\): we would need to delete \\(\\frac{5\\cdot4}{2}=10\\) diagonals, but after removing the \\(k=5\\) diagonals in \\(T\\) there are only \\(n-k=0\\) diagonals left, certainly not enough to delete ten more. The solution implicitly assumes that the \u201clargest\u2011index\u201d diagonals to be deleted are *different* from the diagonals already removed (the ones in \\(T\\)), but when \\(k\\) is large the set of available diagonals is insufficient.  \n\n- Moreover, the solution does not verify that the deleted diagonals are indeed the ones with the largest indices; it merely states \u201cnamely \\(D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots\\)\u201d. If \\(k\\) is close to \\(n\\), some of those indices may already belong to \\(T\\), causing double counting.  \n\n- The condition \\(n\\ge \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}+k\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\)) is required for the described deletion to be possible, but the problem asks for *all* \\(k\\le n\\). The solution does not address the case where this inequality fails.  \n\n**Thus this step contains a serious justification gap.** **Justification Gap\u202f4** (feasibility of deletions for all \\(k\\le n\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 9. Coverage of points on deleted diagonals\n\n> \u201cThe deleted diagonals are not needed any more because every point that lay on them either belongs to a diagonal in\u202fT\u202f\u2026 or belongs to a diagonal whose index is larger than any index kept; such points are covered by the auxiliary vertical/horizontal lines introduced for the earlier diagonals, as shown below.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The argument is sketchy. It claims that a point on a deleted diagonal (which is one of the *largest* diagonals) shares either its \\(x\\)-coordinate or its \\(y\\)-coordinate with a point that already received an auxiliary line.  \n- A rigorous proof would need to show: for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b = t_{\\text{del}}\\) (a deleted index), either the vertical line \\(V_a\\) or the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) was created earlier. The solution does not provide such a proof; it only gives an informal description.  \n- Moreover, the earlier auxiliary lines are created only for points belonging to diagonals in \\(T\\) (which are relatively small). It is not obvious that every large\u2011index diagonal\u2019s points have an \\(x\\) or \\(y\\) that already appeared in a smaller diagonal of \\(T\\). For instance,