ect (i.e., the set\u202f\ud835\udd3d\u202findeed contains exactly the lines described and no other sunny lines appear), the statement follows.  \n- However, because the distinctness and counting of auxiliary lines were not rigorously established, we cannot be certain that no auxiliary line accidentally coincides with a sunny line or with a remaining diagonal. This issue is already covered by Gap\u202f3.  \n\n*No new issue.*\n\n---\n\n### 12. Optimality (no \\(k>n\\))\n\n> \u201cA family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Trivial: there are only \\(n\\) lines in the family, so at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. This part is correct.  \n\n*No issue.*\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Gaps\n\n| Gap | Section / Quote | Nature of the Gap |\n|-----|-----------------|-------------------|\n| 1 | \u201cWhy the rule never uses a line twice.\u201d (vertical lines) | Informal justification that a vertical line cannot be needed again; needs a precise argument. |\n| 2 | Same section (horizontal lines) | Argument that a horizontal line cannot be forced twice is incomplete; does not consider earlier use of the corresponding vertical line. |\n| 3 | Consequence \u201cevery auxiliary line is distinct \u2026\u201d | Relies on the incomplete arguments of Gap\u202f1 and Gap\u202f2; distinctness not rigorously proved. |\n| 4 | Choice of \\(T\\) and deletion of diagonals | No proof that enough diagonals remain to delete for all \\(k\\le n\\); inequality \\(n\\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\) is ignored. |\n| 5 | Coverage of points on deleted diagonals | No rigorous demonstration that every point on a deleted (large\u2011index) diagonal is covered by a previously created vertical/horizontal line. |\n| 6 | Case\u202f3 in covering verification | Uses the same unproved claim as Gap\u202f5; also assumes existence of a suitable \\(t_0\\in T\\). |\n| 7 | Overall distinctness of the final family | Depends on Gap\u202