{t\\in T}(t-2)\\Bigr)\n   =n+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)=0.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 2 (Choice of \\(T\\)).**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) satisfying (9).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the numbers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) as the multiset \\(\\{3\\}\\cup\\{2\\}\\cup\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   The contribution of \\(t=3\\) to the sum (9) is \\(0\\); the contribution of \\(t=2\\) is \\(-1\\); each \\(t\\ge4\\) contributes \\(t-3\\ge1\\).  \n\n   Take the first \\(k\\) elements of the ordered list  \n\n   \\[\n   2,\\;3,\\;4,\\;5,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(k=0\\) we set \\(T=\\varnothing\\) and (9) holds trivially.  \n   If \\(k\\ge1\\) we put  \n\n   \\[\n   T=\\{2,3,4,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)=(-1)+\\sum_{t=3}^{k+1}(t-3)=(-1)+\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k=1\\) the sum equals \\(-1\\); for \\(k\\ge2\\) it equals \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}-1\\).  \n   In both cases we can *compensate* the non\u2011zero value by deleting exactly the same number of *remaining* diagonals (those with the largest indices) from the family \\(\\mathcal D\\).  Deleting a diagonal reduces the right\u2011hand side of (8) by \\(1\\) (because the diagonal contributes \\(+1\\) to the count of lines).  Hence after deleting  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=\\Bigl|\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)\\Bigr|\n   \\]\n\n   diagonals we obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines.  Since the largest index present in \\(\\mathcal D\\) is \\(n+1\\), and we delete at most \\(d\\le\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\le n-1\\) diagonals, the required diagonals always exist. \u220e\n\n   With the set \\(T\\) supplied by Lemma\u202f2 we therefore obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines**.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(t\\notin T\\) and the diagonal \\(D_t\\) has **not** been deleted.*  \n   Then \\(D_t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *Case 2 \u2013 \\(t\\in T\\).*  \n   If \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).  \n   Otherwise \\((a,b)\\in P_t\\); by construction it is covered by the auxiliary line (vertical or horizontal) attached to it, and that line belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Case 3 \u2013 \\(t\\notin T\\) but the diagonal \\(D_t\\) was **deleted**.*  \n   Deletions are performed only on diagonals with the largest indices; denote the set of deleted indices by \\(D_{\\text{del}}\\).  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\in D_{\\text{del}}\\) we have \\(a\\le t-2\\).  \n   When the smallest index \\(t_0\\in T\\) satisfying \\(t_0\\le a+b\\) is processed, the point \\((a,b)\\) either equals \\((t_0-1,1)\\) (already covered by \\(S_{t_0}\\)) or belongs to \\(P_{t_0}\\).  In the latter situation the allocation rule attached to \\((a,b)\\) the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is still present because vertical lines are never removed) or the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (also never removed).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered by an auxiliary line that remains in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   Thus **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n6. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   By construction the only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\).  Hence exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n7. **Optimality.**  \n   A family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) is possible.  \n\n   Consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb N,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_t\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t\\) for a unique \\(t\\) in the above range, hence lies on exactly one diagonal.  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(n\\) distinct (non\u2011sunny) lines; this gives the case \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Replacing diagonals\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a set \\(T\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(|T|=k\\) as described in Lemma\u202f2; let  \n\n\\[\nd:=\\Bigl|\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)\\Bigr|\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n(the number of diagonals that will later be deleted).  \nBecause \\(d\\le n-1\\), the required diagonals exist.\n\nFor each \\(t\\in T\\) we construct:\n\n* a sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  S_t:\\;y-1=m_t\\bigl(x-(t-1)\\bigr),\n  \\tag{16}\n  \\]\n\n  where \\(m_t\\) is any rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); the point \\((t-1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(S_t\\);\n\n* for every \\((i,t-i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t-2\\) an auxiliary line, chosen by the allocation rule described in Lemma\u202f1.  \n  By Lemma\u202f1 the auxiliary lines are all distinct; denote the whole set of them by \\(\\mathcal A\\).\n\nNow delete from \\(\\mathcal D\\)\n\n* all diagonals \\(D_t\\) with \\(t\\in T\\);\n* additionally any \\(d\\) diagonals with the largest indices among the remaining ones (these are the diagonals that are not needed any more, as shown below).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\bigr)\n\\setminus\\{\\text{the }d\\text{ largest remaining diagonals}\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\mathcal A .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting\n\nFrom (14) we start with \\(n\\) lines.  \nWe remove \\(|T|+d=k+d\\) diagonals and add \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\\) auxiliary lines.  Hence\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F|\n=n-(k+d)+k+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)\n=n-d+\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-3)\n=n-d+\\bigl(\\pm d\\bigr)=n .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Distinctness\n\n*The diagonals that remain* are pairwise distinct and none coincides with a sunny line (different slopes) nor with a vertical/horizontal line (different equations).  \n\n*The sunny lines* \\(S_t\\) have distinct slopes \\(m_t\\) (choose them distinct), so they are pairwise different and also different from any vertical or horizontal line.  \n\n*The auxiliary lines* are vertical lines \\(x=i\\) or horizontal lines \\(y=j\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each such line is used at most once, therefore all auxiliary lines are distinct from each other.  \nA vertical (resp. horizontal) auxiliary line cannot coincide with a sunny line because a sunny line is never parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (resp. \\(x\\)-axis).  \nFinally a vertical (resp. horizontal) auxiliary line cannot coincide with a remaining diagonal because a diagonal has slope \\(-1\\).  \n\nHence all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Covering property\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin T\\) and \\(D_t\\) was not deleted*, then \\(D_t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n*If \\(t\\in T\\)*, then either \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) (covered by \\(S_t\\)) or \\((a,b)\\in P_t\\) and is covered by its auxiliary line, which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin T\\) but \\(D_t\\) was deleted*, then \\(t\\) is among the largest indices removed.  \nWhen the smallest index \\(t_0\\in T\\) with \\(t_0\\le t\\) is processed, the point \\((a,b)\\) either equals \\((t_0-1,1)\\) (already covered) or belongs to \\(P_{t_0}\\).  \nIn the latter case the allocation rule attached to \\((a,b)\\) the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (if still unused) or the horizontal line \\(H_b\\); both lines survive all later deletions, so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sunny lines count\n\nThe only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\); consequently exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nholds for every \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e"
