t \\((i,t-i)\\) we first check whether the vertical line \\(V_i\\) has already been used for a previous point.  \n   – If not, we assign \\(V_i\\) to \\((i,t-i)\\).  \n   – If \\(V_i\\) is already used, we assign the horizontal line \\(H_{t-i}\\).\n\n   Because we treat the diagonals in increasing order of \\(t\\), a vertical line \\(V_i\\) can be used at most once (it can only appear on the diagonal whose sum of coordinates equals the smallest possible \\(t\\) containing that \\(i\\)).  \n   Similarly a horizontal line \\(H_j\\) can be used at most once: if two points \\((i_1,j)\\) and \\((i_2,j)\\) with \\(i_1\\neq i_2\\) required \\(H_j\\), the one belonging to the smaller diagonal would have taken its vertical line instead.  \n   Hence **Lemma 1** holds:\n\n   > **Lemma 1.** The allocation rule assigns to each point of \\(\\bigcup_{t\\in T}P_t\\) a distinct line, and every such line is either a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n\n3. **The final family of lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\n   \\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{\\text{all auxiliary lines produced by the allocation rule}\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   \\(\\mathcal D\\) contains \\(n\\) lines.  We delete exactly \\(|T|=k\\) diagonals and add \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The allocation rule creates, for each \\(t\\in T\\), exactly \\(t-2\\) auxiliary lines; altogether  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-2)=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   new lines.  Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F|\n   =\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+k+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n   =n+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we must delete the surplus \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) lines.  \n   We do this by **removing** the \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) *largest* remaining diagonals (those with the greatest indices).  \n   Since the largest index among the original diagonals is \\(n+1\\), and we delete at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n-1\\qquad (k\\le n),\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   there are always enough diagonals to delete.  \n   After this deletion the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(t\\notin T\\) and the diagonal \\(D_t\\) was **not** deleted.*  \n   Then \\(D_t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(t\\in T\\).*  \n   If \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).  \n   Otherwise \\((a,b)\\in P_t\\) and, by construction, is covered by the auxiliary line (vertical or horizontal) assigned to it; that line belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Case 3 – \\(t\\notin T\\) but \\(D_t\\) was deleted.*  \n   Deletions affect only diagonals with the largest indices, say \\(t\\geq n+2-\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\).  \n   For such a point we consider the smallest index \\(t_0\\in T\\) with \\(t_0\\le t\\) (such a \\(t_0\\) exists because \\(2\\in T\\)).  \n   The point \\((a,b)\\) either equals \\((t_0-1,1)\\) (already covered by \\(S_{t_0}\\)) or belongs to \\(P_{t_0}\\); in the latter situation the allocation rule attached to \\((a,b)\\) a vertical line \\(V_a\\) or a horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n   Neither vertical nor horizontal lines are ever removed, so \\((a,b)\\) is still covered.\n\n   Hence **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n5. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   By construction the only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\).  \n   Consequently exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Optimality.**  \n   A family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb N,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(D_t\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t\\) for a unique \\(t\\) in the above range, hence lies on exactly one diagonal.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(n\\) distinct (non‑sunny) lines; this gives the case \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\in T\\) we replace the diagonal \\(D_t\\) by the following collection of lines.\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nChoose a rational slope \\(m_t\\neq0,-1\\) (e.g. \\(m_t=2\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y-1=m_t\\bigl(x-(t-1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((t-1,1)\\) satisfies \\((t-1)+1=t\\le n+1\\); hence \\((t-1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and lies on \\(S_t\\).  \nBecause the slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(S_t\\) is sunny.\n\n*Auxiliary lines.*  \nThe remaining points of \\(D_t\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_t=\\{(i,t-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le t-2\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\((i,t-i)\\in P_t\\) we assign a line according to the **allocation rule**:\n\nprocess the indices \\(t\\) in increasing order; when a point \\((i,t-i)\\) is encountered,\n- if the vertical line \\(V_i:x=i\\) has not been used before, assign \\(V_i\\);\n- otherwise assign the horizontal line \\(H_{t-i}:y=t-i\\).\n\nBecause we never reuse a vertical line (it can appear only on the smallest diagonal containing that \\(i\\)) and we never reuse a horizontal line (otherwise the earlier point would have taken its vertical line), the rule yields distinct lines. This is formalised as:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** The allocation rule assigns to each point of \\(\\bigcup_{t\\in T}P_t\\) a distinct line, each of which is either a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a vertical line \\(V_i\\). It can be assigned only to a point whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\); such points lie on diagonals \\(D_{i+(t-i)}=D_t\\) with varying \\(t\\). The smallest possible sum is \\(t=i+1\\), which belongs to \\(T\\) (since \\(2\\le i+1\\le k+1\\)). When we reach this smallest diagonal, \\(V_i\\) is still unused and is assigned; later diagonals have larger sums, so the rule will find \\(V_i\\) already used and will therefore assign the horizontal alternative. Hence \\(V_i\\) is used at most once.  \n\nFor a horizontal line \\(H_j\\), suppose it were assigned to two distinct points \\((i_1,j)\\) and \\((i_2,j)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\). The point with smaller sum of coordinates belongs to the diagonal with index \\(t_1=i_1+j\\), which is processed earlier. At that moment \\(V_{i_1}\\) is still free, so the rule would have assigned \\(V_{i_1}\\) rather than \\(H_j\\), a contradiction. Thus each horizontal line is also used at most once. ∎\n\nConsequently the set of auxiliary lines produced for all \\(t\\in T\\) consists of exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-2)=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The final family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\text{all auxiliary lines from Lemma 1}\\}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nInitially \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\).  We have removed \\(|T|=k\\) diagonals and added \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) auxiliary lines, so  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F|=n-k+k+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}=n+\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nTo obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we delete the surplus \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) *largest* remaining diagonals (those with the greatest indices).  \nSince the largest index among the original diagonals is \\(n+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n-1\\qquad(k\\le n),\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nthere are always enough diagonals to delete.  After this deletion the family still contains the sunny lines \\(S_t\\) and all auxiliary lines, and its cardinality is exactly \\(n\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct: the remaining diagonals have slope \\(-1\\); the sunny lines have slopes \\(m_t\\neq0,-1\\) and are not vertical; the auxiliary lines are vertical or horizontal, never coinciding with each other by Lemma 1, nor with any diagonal or sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Covering property\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(t=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin T\\) and \\(D_t\\) was not deleted*, then \\(D_t\\in\\mathcal F\\) and the point lies on it.\n\n*If \\(t\\in T\\)*, then either \\((a,b)=(t-1,1)\\) (covered by \\(S_t\\)) or \\((a,b)\\in P_t\\); the latter case is covered by the auxiliary line assigned to that point, which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\notin T\\) but \\(D_t\\) was deleted*, then \\(t\\) is among the largest indices removed.  \nLet \\(t_0\\) be the smallest element of \\(T\\) with \\(t_0\\le t\\) (such a \\(t_0\\) exists because \\(2\\in T\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)=(t_0-1,1)\\) we are done. Otherwise \\((a,b)\\in P_{t_0}\\); by the allocation rule it is covered by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=b\\), both of which remain in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Number of sunny lines\n\nThe only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\); therefore exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎"
