ver reuse a horizontal line (otherwise the earlier point would have taken its vertical line), the rule yields distinct lines. This is formalised as:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** The allocation rule assigns to each point of \\(\\bigcup_{t\\in T}P_t\\) a distinct line, each of which is either a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a vertical line \\(V_i\\). It can be assigned only to a point whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\); such points lie on diagonals \\(D_{i+(t-i)}=D_t\\) with varying \\(t\\). The smallest possible sum is \\(t=i+1\\), which belongs to \\(T\\) (since \\(2\\le i+1\\le k+1\\)). When we reach this smallest diagonal, \\(V_i\\) is still unused and is assigned; later diagonals have larger sums, so the rule will find \\(V_i\\) already used and will therefore assign the horizontal alternative. Hence \\(V_i\\) is used at most once.  \n\nFor a horizontal line \\(H_j\\), suppose it were assigned to two distinct points \\((i_1,j)\\) and \\((i_2,j)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\). The point with smaller sum of coordinates belongs to the diagonal with index \\(t_1=i_1+j\\), which is processed earlier. At that moment \\(V_{i_1}\\) is still free, so the rule would have assigned \\(V_{i_1}\\) rather than \\(H_j\\), a contradiction. Thus each horizontal line is also used at most once. ∎\n\nConsequently the set of auxiliary lines produced for all \\(t\\in T\\) consists of exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\in T}(t-2)=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-2)=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The final family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{S_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\text{all auxiliary lines from Lemma 1}\\}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nInitially \\(|\\mathcal D|=n\\).  We have removed \\(|T|=k\\) diagonals and added \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\\) auxiliary lines, so  \n\n\\[\n|