1\\) or \\(n-s=2\\). In other words\n\n\\[\ns\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain three or more sunny lines**; the admissible values of \\(k\\) satisfy \\(k\\le2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Realising \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake only lines parallel to the forbidden directions.  \n\n* For each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\) draw the vertical line \\(x=\\text{const}\\) through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) having that \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \n* For each integer \\(y\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le n-1\\) draw the horizontal line \\(y=\\text{const}\\) through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) having that \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \n* For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) draw the line \\(x+y=s\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) with that sum.\n\nThese three families contain \\(3n-3\\) distinct lines, each of which passes through at least one point of \\(T_n\\). Because a point of \\(T_n\\) may belong to several of the drawn lines, we can discard any surplus lines while still covering all points. By discarding \\(3n-3-n\\) lines we obtain a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Realising \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}x-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\frac{n}{n-1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) is \\((n-1,n-2)\\).  \n\nRemove this point; the remaining \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-1\\) points each have a distinct \\(x\\), a distinct \\(y\\) or a distinct \\(x+y\\) value. As in the \\(k=0\\) case we can cover all of them with at most \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, choosing the lines so that together with \\(L\\) we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Realising \\(k=2\\)\n\nDefine the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y=\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}x-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,},\n\\qquad\nL_2:\\; y=-\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nA short calculation shows  \n\n\\[\nL_1\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}=\\{(n-1,n-2)\\},\\qquad\nL_2\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}=\\{(1,n-1)\\},\n\\]\n\nand both points belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n\nDelete these two points; the set that remains has  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2\n\\]\n\nelements, each with a distinct \\(x\\), \\(y\\) or \\(x+y\\) value. As before we can cover them with at most \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, obtaining a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nThe upper\u2011bound argument forces \\(k\\le2\\); the constructions above show that each of \\(k=0,1,2\\) can indeed be achieved. Therefore the complete set of admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
