ree forbidden values\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  To obtain a configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines one would need\nto combine sunny lines with some of the three non‑sunny directions (horizontal,\nvertical, or slope \\(-1\\)) in such a way that every point of the triangular lattice\n\\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\nA natural idea is to let a sunny line pass through a whole “column’’ of points\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\le n+1-a\\}\\); however a non‑vertical line can intersect a given\nvertical line in at most one point, so a single sunny line cannot cover an entire\ncolumn.  Conversely, a non‑sunny line (e.g. a vertical line) can cover an entire\ncolumn but cannot be used to reduce the number of sunny lines below \\(n\\) without\nleaving uncovered points.\n\nAt present no systematic method for constructing such mixed families has been\nfound, nor has an impossibility proof been obtained.  Consequently the existence\nof configurations for any positive integer \\(k\\) (including the extreme case\n\\(k=n\\)) remains an open question.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nWe have rigorously shown that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is\nadmissible.  The status of all other non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is unresolved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
