lines.  \n- A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\) (otherwise the line would have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Therefore with at most one non\u2011sunny line we could cover at most \\(2(n-1)+1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points for \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradiction shows that at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.\n\n*Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \n- Choose \\(k\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n- Arrange them so that each of these \\(k\\) lines passes through exactly two points of \\(S\\) and the collection of all such points is pairwise disjoint. This is possible because the points of \\(S\\) lie on a triangular lattice and we can select disjoint \u201cedges\u2019\u2019 of that lattice.  \n- The remaining \\((n-k)\\) lines are taken to be non\u2011sunny: we use \\((n-k-2)\\) vertical lines and two horizontal lines (or any other combination of the three forbidden directions) to cover the still uncovered points of \\(S\\).  \n- Because each non\u2011sunny line can cover exactly one point of \\(S\\), we simply assign each uncovered point to a distinct non\u2011sunny line.  \n- The total number of lines used is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) and the construction satisfies the covering condition.  \n- By varying the choice of the \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is precisely that the family of lines covers the finite set \\(S\\).  \nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n} (n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA line that is **not** sunny (hence parallel to one of the three forbidden directions) will be called a **bad line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.** A bad line contains at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is horizontal, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nTwo distinct points of \\(S\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces the sum of the coordinates to be different for any two points with the same \\(y\\).  \nHence a horizontal line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point.  \nThe same argument works for a vertical line (distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (the quantity \\(a-b\\) is constant on such a line, but the values of \\(a-b\\) for points of \\(S\\) are all distinct). \u220e  \n\nConsequently each bad line can cover **exactly one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\).  \nThe slopes between any two of them are equal, so the three points are collinear.  \nIf the common slope were \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) the line would be bad, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nThus the slope of \\(L\\) is different from the three forbidden values, but a line of such a slope can intersect the integer lattice \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) in at most two points whose coordinates satisfy a linear inequality of the form \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nA direct verification (checking the possible differences of the coordinates) shows that three points of \\(S\\) cannot be collinear unless the line has one of the forbidden slopes. \u220e  \n\nHence every sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet the configuration contain \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(b=n-s\\) bad lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the bad lines together cover at most \\(b\\) points of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f2 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2s+b = 2s+(n-s)=s+n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le s+n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\n      =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\le n\\), inequality (2) can hold only when \\(s\\le n-2\\).  \nIndeed, if \\(s=n-1\\) then the right\u2011hand side of (2) equals \\(n-1+n=n+ (n-1)=2n-1\\), while the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge \\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); a direct check shows \\(2n-1<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nSimilarly \\(s=n\\) is impossible.  \n\nThus we have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction.**  \n\n1. **Choosing the sunny lines.**  \n   Select \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a pair of points of \\(S\\) that lie on a line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\).  \n   Because the points of \\(S\\) form a triangular lattice, we can choose these pairs so that the \\(2k\\) points are all different.  \n   (For instance, take the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); the line through them has slope \\(0\\), which is forbidden, so instead take \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i,2)\\) \u2013 this line has slope \\(\\infty\\), also forbidden.  \n   To avoid the forbidden slopes we may take \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\); the slope is \\(\\frac{1}{1}=1\\), which is allowed.  \n   By varying the second coordinate we obtain \\(k\\) admissible slopes and \\(2k\\) distinct points.)  \n\n   Denote by \\(L_i\\) the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) passing through the chosen pair; each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and covers exactly those two points.\n\n2. **Covering the remaining points.**  \n   After step\u202f1, exactly \\(|S|-2k\\) points of \\(S\\) remain uncovered.  \n   Set \\(b=n-k\\). Because of (3) we have \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Use \\(b-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=c_j\\) (with distinct integer abscissae) and two horizontal lines \\(y=d_1, y=d_2\\) to cover the remaining points, assigning one distinct line to each uncovered point.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each such line meets \\(S\\) in at most one point, so we can indeed allocate a different line to each uncovered point.  \n   Since the number of uncovered points equals \\(|S|-2k\\le b\\) (inequality (2) with \\(s=k\\) gives \\(|S|\\le k+n\\), i.e. \\(|S|-2k\\le n-k=b\\)), there are enough bad lines to cover them all.\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   - The total number of lines is \\(k+(b)=k+(n-k)=n\\).  \n   - Exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny; the remaining \\(b\\) lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence are not sunny.  \n   - Every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines by construction.  \n   - All lines are distinct because the sunny lines have distinct slopes, and the bad lines have distinct positions (different \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinates).\n\nThus for each \\(k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the upper bound (3) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\