            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} L\\supseteq S .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **bad** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (bad lines)\n\nA bad line contains at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is horizontal, all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nFor two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S\\) we have \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces the sum \\(a+b\\) to be different for points with the same \\(y\\).  \nHence a horizontal line meets \\(S\\) in at most one point.  \n\nThe same argument works for a vertical line (different \\(x\\)-coordinates) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\): on such a line the quantity \\(a-b\\) is constant, but the values of \\(a-b\\) for points of \\(S\\) are all distinct, so at most one point can lie on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\).  \nSince the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\n   =\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}=m .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m=0\\) the line is horizontal; if \\(m=\\infty\\) it is vertical; if \\(m=-1\\) it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nAll three possibilities contradict the hypothesis that \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\); consequently it contains at most two. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration satisfying (1) contain \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(b=n-s\\) bad lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the bad lines together cover at most \\(b\\) points of \\(S\\); by Lemma 2 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2s+b = s+n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le s+n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration has at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 – sunny lines.**  \nChoose the slope \\(\\alpha=1\\) (any value different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) would work).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1\\le n,\\qquad 1+ i\\le n,\\qquad (i+1)+2\\le n+1\\;(i\\le n-2),\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for all \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe line \\(L_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{(i+1)-i}=1\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(2k\\) points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) are pairwise distinct, hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct and each covers exactly its two designated points.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 – bad lines.**  \nAfter Step 1 the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nU=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\},\n\\qquad |U|=|S|-2k.\n\\]\n\nSet \\(b=n-k\\).  From (3) with \\(s=k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|-2k\\le b .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the number of uncovered points does not exceed \\(b\\).\n\nTake \\(b\\) distinct bad lines as follows:\n\n* \\(b-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=c_1,\\dots ,x=c_{b-2}\\) where the integers \\(c_j\\) are chosen so that each line meets at most one point of \\(U\\);\n* two horizontal lines \\(y=d_1,\\;y=d_2\\) with distinct integer ordinates, also chosen to meet at most one point of \\(U\\).\n\nBecause a bad line can intersect \\(S\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1), we can assign a different uncovered point of \\(U\\) to each of the \\(b\\) bad lines.  Condition (5) guarantees that there are enough bad lines for all points of \\(U\\).\n\nDenote the resulting bad lines by \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_b\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 – verification.**  \n\n*Number of lines.*  We have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(b=n-k\\) bad lines \\(M_j\\); altogether \\(k+b=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Sunny count.*  Exactly the \\(L_i\\) are sunny, so the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  Every point of \\(S\\) is either one of the \\(P_i,Q_i\\) (covered by a sunny line) or belongs to \\(U\\) (covered by the bad line assigned to it).  Hence (1) holds.\n\n*Distinctness.*  The sunny lines have the same slope but different intercepts, so they are distinct.  The bad lines are distinct because they have different equations (\\(x=c_j\\) with distinct \\(c_j\\) and \\(y=d_1\\neq y=d_2\\)).  No sunny line coincides with a bad line because their slopes differ.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion  \n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, while the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
