            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (covering a triangular lattice with slope‑\\(1\\) lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le m+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis covered by the \\(m\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\((x,y)\\in T_m\\).  Because \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+y\\le m+1\\),\n\n\\[\n0\\le x-y\\le m-1 .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(d:=x-y\\).  Then \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) and \\(y = x-d\\).  Choosing\n\\(i:=d+1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\)) we obtain \\(y = x-i+1\\), i.e.\n\\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one\n\\(\\ell_i\\).  The lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Vertical (non‑sunny) lines.**  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines covering the remaining points.**  \n   Consider the subset of \\(T_n\\) consisting of points whose first coordinate is\n   at least \\(n-k+1\\):\n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\((a,b)\\in S\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   a' := a-(n-k),\\qquad b' := b .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   a'+b' = (a-(n-k))+b = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\).  Hence \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(T_k\\) is covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   Translating these lines back to the original coordinates gives\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;b = (a-(n-k))-i+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;b = a-(n-k+i-1),\n   \\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(+1\\), therefore it is sunny, and the collection\n   \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers exactly the set \\(S\\).\n\n3. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\), then it lies on the vertical\n     line \\(V_a\\).\n   * If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in S\\) and, as shown above, lies on one of the\n     lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The lines \\(V_j\\) are vertical, hence not sunny.  The lines \\(L_i\\) have slope\n   \\(+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, and the sunny lines have\n   distinct intercepts (they differ by the constant term \\(-(n-k+i-1)\\)).\n   Hence all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are distinct.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), we have\nproduced, for each such \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Statement\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThat is, for every \\(k\\) in this range there exists a configuration of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
