bb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le m+1\\text{ and }x\\ge y\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m)\n\\]\n\ncover \\(T_m^{\\ge}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((x,y)\\in T_m^{\\ge}\\).  Because \\(x\\ge y\\) we have \\(d:=x-y\\ge 0\\).  \nFrom \\(x+y\\le m+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx-y = d \\le (x+y)-2y \\le (m+1)-2\\cdot 1 = m-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).  Setting \\(i=d+1\\) gives \\(y = x-i+1\\), i.e. the point lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct lines because the intercepts differ. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-k)\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y = x-(n-k+i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the lines.*\n\n* Each \\(V_j\\) is vertical, hence not sunny.\n* Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(+1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.\n* All lines are distinct: the \\(V_j\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, the \\(L_i\\) have different constant terms \\(-(n-k+i-1)\\).\n\n*Covering proof.*\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nDefine \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b' = b\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n\\]\nand clearly \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\).  \nMoreover \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) implies \\(a'\\ge 1\\), and because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\) we have \\(a'\\ge b'\\).  \nHence \\((a',b')\\in T_k^{\\ge}\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1, \\((a',b')\\) lies on some \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb' = a' - i + 1 .\n\\]\n\nReturning to the original coordinates,\n\n\\[\nb = (a-(n-k)) - i + 1 = a-(n-k+i-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\] ∎"
