wing families of lines.\n\n*Vertical (non‑sunny) lines*  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm:=k,\\qquad r:=\\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\; s:=\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA_i:\\;y = x-(n-k+i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,r),\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nB_i:\\;y = 2x-(n-k+i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,s).\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{A_1,\\dots ,A_r,B_1,\\dots ,B_s\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (3) have slope \\(+1\\), all lines in (4) have slope \\(+2\\);\nneither slope equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every line in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is\nsunny except the vertical ones.  The constant terms\n\\(-(n-k+i-1)\\) are distinct, hence all lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Coverage proof\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Assume \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)*.  Put  \n\n\\[\na' = a-(n-k),\\qquad b' = b .\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(n-k)\\le k+1,\n\\]\nand clearly \\(1\\le a',b'\\le k\\).  Thus \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).\n\n- If \\(a'\\ge b'\\) then \\((a',b')\\in T_k^{\\ge}\\); by Lemma 1 it lies on\n  the line \\(A_{a'-b'+1}\\), which is one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n  Translating back gives that \\((a,b)\\) lies on that same line.\n\n- If \\(a'< b'\\) then \\((a',b')\\in T_k^{<}\\); by Lemma 2 it lies on\n  the line \\(B_{b'-a'+1}\\), also belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Again\n  the translation shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on this line.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Counting sunny lines\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny)\nand \\(r+s=k\\) lines of slopes \\(+1\\) or \\(+2\\) (sunny).  Ther