ng points of \\(T_n\\) are\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the points with \\(a+b=s\\) form a diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n-2\\) such diagonals, namely those with sums \\(s=3,4,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Adding \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in U\\) lying on distinct diagonals (i.e. with distinct values of \\(a_i+b_i\\)).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line \\(\\ell_i\\) to be the unique line through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{b_i}{a_i}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 2\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty\\).  \nMoreover \\(m_i\\neq -1\\) because \\(\\frac{b_i}{a_i}>0\\).  \nHence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct because the points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) lie on different diagonals, so the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) covers its defining point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) (and possibly other points of \\(U\\)); in any case all points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) are now covered.\n\n#### 2.4  Adding the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nThere remain exactly \\((n-2)-k\\) diagonals of slope \\(-1\\) that have not been used in the previous step.  \nFor each such diagonal \\(D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) (with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\)) choose a point \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) that is still uncovered (such a point exists because each diagonal contains at least two points and at most one of them could have been used to define a sunny line).  \n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}: x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are non\u2011sunny.  \nBecause the chosen points lie on distinct diagonals, the lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\) are distinct.  \nEach \\(\\ell_{s}\\) covers its chosen point, and consequently every point of the diagonal \\(D_s\\) is covered (the whole diagonal lies on \\(\\ell_{s}\\)).\n\n#### 2.5  Verification\n\nWe have produced\n\n* the two initial non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\);\n* \\(n-2-k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\).\n\nAltogether there are \\(2+k+(n-2-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \nBy construction every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them:\n\n* points on the first row or first column are on \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\);\n* points on a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) are on the corresponding line \\(\\ell_{s}\\);\n* the finitely many points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) used to define the sunny lines are on those sunny lines.\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny, while all the others are parallel to one of the forbidden directions.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom Part\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom Part\u202f2 we have the sufficient condition that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
