ingle Sunny Line  \n\n#### Step 1.1 – Claim about the number of direction families  \n> “Because there are only three families of non‑sunny directions, at most two of them can appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\); otherwise we would have at least three non‑sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis that only one line is sunny.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe hypothesis only restricts the *number of sunny lines* to one; it places **no restriction** on how many non‑sunny lines may be used. One may have many vertical lines, many horizontal lines, and many diagonal lines simultaneously. The statement “at most two of them can appear” is therefore false. This is a **Critical Error** because it eliminates an entire class of possible configurations (those using all three families) from consideration without justification.\n\n#### Step 1.2 – Case A (vertical + horizontal, no diagonal)  \n\n> “All points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). … Consequently each such point must be covered by the unique sunny line. … Thus at most two points of the diagonal are covered – contradiction.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) indeed contains the points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=i\\) meets this diagonal exactly at \\((i,n+1-i)\\); a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) meets it at \\((n+1-j,j)\\).  \nSince the configuration is allowed to contain **many** vertical and horizontal lines (one for each admissible \\(i\\) or \\(j\\)), every point of the diagonal can be covered by a distinct vertical or horizontal line. The argument that “each such point must be covered by the unique sunny line” is therefore false. Consequently the derived bound “at most two points” is incorrect. This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates Case A.\n\n#### Step 1.3 – Case B (vertical + diagonal, no horizontal)  \n\n> “Consider the set \\(H=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset S_n\\). … Hence at most two points of \\(H\\) are covered, whereas \\(|H|=n\\ge 3\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe set \\(H\\) consists of \\(n\\) points, each with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \nA vertical line \\(x=i\\) covers exactly the point \\((i,1)\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can cover at most one point of \\(H\\) (the one with \\(i=c-1\\)).  \nThe solution assumes **only one** vertical line and **only one** diagonal line are present, leading to the “at most two points” claim. However, the family may contain many vertical lines (e.g., \\(x=1, x=2, \\dots\\)), each covering a different point of \\(H\\). Hence the bound is unjustified. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n#### Step 1.4 – Case C (horizontal + diagonal)  \n\nThe argument is symmetric to Case B and suffers from the same flaw. **Critical Error**.\n\n#### Consequence of Section 1  \n\nBecause the impossibility proof for \\(k=1\\) rests on the three flawed cases, the conclusion “\\(k\\neq1\\)” is **not proved**. The solution therefore lacks a valid argument for the exclusion of \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Construction with No Sunny Lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe solution proposes the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n\\}.\n\\]\n\n- Count: \\(|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal D|=(n-2)+(n-2)+1=n\\).  \n- All lines are non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, or diagonal).\n\n**Covering argument:**  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\):\n\n1. If \\(a\\le n-2\\) → vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains it.  \n2. If \\(a=n-1\\) → then \\(b\\le 1\\); the only possible point is \\((n-1,1)\\), which satisfies \\(a+b=n\\) and lies on \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \n3. If \\(a=n\\) → then \\(b=0\\), which is not in \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. This part is **correct**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. “Replacement operation” – Supposed construction of even \\(k\\)\n\nThe operation is described as:\n\n- Choose an unused index \\(i\\) ( \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) ) and set \\(j=n-i\\).\n- Remove the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n- Insert a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\((i,j)\\) with a new slope.\n\n**Claim:** “Any other point that was on the removed vertical line \\(x=i\\) … is still on another vertical line \\(x=i'\\) … because we removed only one vertical and one horizontal line.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nA point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq j\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) **only**. After removing \\(x=i\\), there is no other vertical line with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; all remaining vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-values. Hence such a point becomes uncovered unless it also lies on the newly added sunny line, which it does not (the sunny line passes through only \\((i,j)\\)). Therefore the covering property is **lost**. The argument that “another vertical line” exists is false. This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates the entire construction for even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\nBecause the operation fails, the claim that all even \\(k\\) up to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) are attainable is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction of odd \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\nThe method builds on the (flawed) even‑\\(k\\) configurations:\n\n- Start from a configuration with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.\n- Add a new sunny line through a point on a non‑sunny line.\n- Delete a non‑sunny line that supposedly does not contain a uniquely covered point.\n\n**Problematic claim:** “Every non‑sunny line contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\); at least one of those points also lies on another line (for instance, any point on a vertical line also lies on a horizontal line with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nAfter the (incorrect) replacements of Section 3, many vertical or horizontal lines may have been removed, so a given vertical line may no longer have a corresponding horizontal line sharing a point. The statement that “any point on a vertical line also lies on a horizontal line with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate” presumes the presence of that