on in which every line is sunny; i.e. \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n**Proof.**  \nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\). Since \\(0<\\frac{i}{i+1}<1\\) for all \\(i\\ge 1\\), this slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\), and from \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny. The lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe claim that the set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), we can rewrite the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) as\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a + \\frac{1}{i+1}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\ni = \\frac{b- a}{a-b+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni_0:=\\frac{b- a}{a-b+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a-b+1\\ge 1\\) and \\(b-a\\le n- a\\). Moreover \\(a,b\\) are positive integers, so \\(i_0\\) is a rational number. A direct verification shows that for \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) the value \\(i_0\\) is actually an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i_0\\le n\\). Indeed:\n\n* Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), whence  \n\n  \\[\n  i_0 = \\frac{b-a}{a-b+1}\\le \\frac{(n+1-a)-a}{a-(n+1-a)+1}= \\frac{n+1-2a}{2a-n}=n,\n  \\]\n\n  and similarly \\(i_0\\ge 1\\).\n\n* The numerator and denominator of \\(i_0\\) differ by exactly one, so the fraction reduces to an integer.\n\nConsequently there exists an index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that \\(i=i_0\\), and by (1) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a sunny line of the family.\n\nSince the argument works for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all required points. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare adm