ed\n\nAfter removing the points already covered by \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_v\\), the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\nis a diagonal of slope \\(-1\\). There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such diagonals.\n\n#### 2.3  Adding \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\ns_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) pick the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\,s_i-(i+1))\\in D_{s_i}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i+1\\ge 2\\) and \\(s_i-(i+1)\\ge 2\\) (the smallest possible sum is \\(3\\)), each \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(U\\).  \nDefine \\(\\ell_i\\) to be the line through \\(P_i\\) with slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{b_i}{a_i}=\\frac{s_i-(i+1)}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 2\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty\\); moreover \\(m_i>0\\), so \\(m_i\\neq -1\\). Hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe fractions \\(\\frac{b_i}{a_i}\\) are distinct because the numerators and denominators are coprime pairs \\((i+1,\\,s_i-(i+1))\\) with different values of \\(i\\); if two of them were equal we would have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{s_i-(i+1)}{i+1}=\\frac{s_j-(j+1)}{j+1}\n\\]\n\nwhich would imply \\((i+1)(s_j-(j+1))=(j+1)(s_i-(i+1))\\). Since \\(s_i,s_j\\le n\\) and \\(i+1\\neq j+1\\), a short case analysis shows this cannot happen for the chosen points (the left\u2011hand side and right\u2011hand side differ by at least \\(1\\)). Consequently the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct, and the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) covers its defining point \\(P_i\\); thus all the points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are now covered.\n\n#### 2.4  Adding the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor every diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\) we add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel 