"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof of the existence part for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWhat I can establish rigorously is the *necessary* condition  \n\\[\nk\\le n-2,\n\\]\nand I give a correct construction that works for the extremal value \\(k=0\\) (i.e. when all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny).  \nThus I present a *partial* solution: the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is proved, and the case \\(k=0\\) is shown to be attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Necessary bound** \u2013 Using a counting argument on the \u201cborder\u201d points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) of the set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   we prove that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) must contain at least two lines that are parallel to one of the forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)). Consequently at most \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\)** \u2013 Take the two coordinate axes \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) (horizontal and vertical) together with the \\(n-2\\) lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_s:\\; x+y=s\\qquad (s=3,4,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct, there are exactly \\(n\\) of them, and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on one of them. Hence the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\nThe remaining values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) are left open; a correct construction for those cases has not been obtained.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the two subsets of \\(T_n\\)\n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nV=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth consist of points that lie on the \u201cbottom\u201d and \u201cleft\u201d edges of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nObserve that any line which contains two distinct points of \\(H\\) must be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)); any line which contains two distinct points of \\(V\\) must be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \nA line that contains a point of \\(H\\) and a point of \\(V\\) must have slope \\(-1\\), because for \\((a,1)\\in H\\) and \\((1,b)\\in V\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{1-a}= -1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b=2.\n\\]\n\nSince the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=2\\) is \\((1,1)\\), the only line that meets both \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) in points of \\(T_n\\) is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and any other point of \\(T_n\\); such a line has slope \\(-1\\) exactly when the second point also satisfies \\(a+b=2\\), which never occurs. Hence **no** line of the covering can contain simultaneously a point of \\(H\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and a point of \\(V\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\).\n\nConsequently, to cover all points of \\(H\\) we need at least \\(\\lceil |H|/2\\rceil =\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) distinct lines, and similarly to cover all points of \\(V\\) we need at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) distinct lines. Since a single line can cover at most one point of \\(H\\) and at most one point of \\(V\\) unless it is horizontal or vertical, the total number of lines needed to cover \\(H\\cup V\\) is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\ge 2.\n\\]\n\nThus **any** family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) must contain at least two lines that are either horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); all such lines are *non\u2011sunny*. Therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* the horizontal line \\(\\ell_h:\\; y=1\\);\n* the vertical line \\(\\ell_v:\\; x=1\\);\n* for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the line  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_s:\\; x+y=s .\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines.\n\nNow take any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_h\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_v\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\); then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(3\\le s\\le n\\), and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=s\\), i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) for any admissible configuration, and we have exhibited a concrete configuration achieving \\(k=0\\). The existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains open. Therefore the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is not fully determined by the present work."

