ing \\(n-h-v\\) lines are neither horizontal nor vertical, so they can\ncover at most \\(2(n-h-v)\\) points of \\(H\\cup V\\).  Altogether we must have  \n\n\\[\nh\\cdot n+v\\cdot n+2\\,(n-h-v)\\;\\ge\\;2n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h\\le n\\) and \\(v\\le n\\), the left\u2011hand side is at most \\(2n+2(n-h-v)\\).\nThus we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2n+2(n-h-v)\\ge 2n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad h+v\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently **any** covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at least two\nlines that are horizontal, vertical or (by the same argument applied to the\nother two sides of the triangle) of slope \\(-1\\).  All three directions are\nnon\u2011sunny, so at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A base configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{h}: y=1\\)   (horizontal);\n* \\(\\ell_{v}: x=1\\)   (vertical);\n* for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the line  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_{s}: x+y=s .\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence\nnone of them is sunny.  There are exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{h}\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{v}\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(3\\le s\\le n\\),\n  and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=s\\), i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_{s}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and all\n\\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Replacing a diagonal line by two sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  \nThe diagonal line \\(\\ell_{s}: x+y=s\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{s}^{(1)} &: y=-2x+(s+1),\\\\[2pt]\n\\ell_{s}^{(2)} &: y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{s+1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell_{s}^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_{s}^{(2)}\\) are sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Every integer point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) lies on exactly one of the\ntwo lines in (4.1).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nWrite \\(b=s-a\\).  Substituting into the equations of the two lines gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ns-a &= -2a+(s+1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a=1,\\\\[2pt]\ns-a &= -\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{s+1}{2}\n      \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a\\equiv 0\\pmod 2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the point with \\(a=1\\) (i.e. \\((1,s-1)\\)) lies on \\(\\ell_{s}^{(1)}\\); all\nremaining points have even abscissa and satisfy the second equation, hence lie\non \\(\\ell_{s}^{(2)}\\).  The two lines are distinct, so each point of the diagonal\nbelongs to exactly one of them. \u220e\n\nConsequently the pair \\(\\{\\ell_{s}^{(1)},\\ell_{s}^{(2)}\\}\\) covers **the same**\nset of points of \\(T_n\\) as the original diagonal line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the base configuration of Section\u202f3 (all \\(n\\) lines non\u2011sunny).\nWe perform the following operation repeatedly.\n\n*Choose a diagonal line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) (with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\)).*  \n*Delete \\(\\ell_{s}\\) and insert the two sunny lines \\(\\ell_{s}^{(1)}\\) and\n\\(\\ell_{s}^{(2)}\\) defined in (4.1).*\n\nThe operation increases the total number of lines by one and increases the\nnumber of sunny lines by two (the deleted line was non\u2011sunny).  To keep the total\nnumber of lines equal to \\(n\\) we simultaneously delete **one** other non\u2011sunny\nline (any horizontal,