            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the legs).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider the three sides of the triangle:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nS_2&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nS_3&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach side contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points, and the three sides are\npairwise disjoint.\n\n*If a line is sunny, it can meet each side in at most one lattice point.*  \nIndeed, if a sunny line contained two distinct points of, say, \\(S_1\\) then it would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny; the same argument works for \\(S_2\\) (vertical) and for \\(S_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nLet a covering family consist of \\(h\\) horizontal lines, \\(v\\) vertical lines,\n\\(d\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) and \\(s\\) sunny lines; thus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d+s=n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_1\\cup S_2\\cup S_3\\) must be covered.  \nThe horizontal lines together can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_1\\) (they\ncover the whole side) and none of \\(S_2\\) or \\(S_3\\).  \nSimilarly, the vertical lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_2\\), and the\n\\(d\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nA sunny line can contribute at most one point to each side, i.e. at most three\npoints in total.  Hence the \\(s\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3s\\) points\namong the three sides.\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nn+n+n\\;\\le\\; n\\;(h+v+d)+3s .\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n3n\\;\\le\\; n\\,(n-s)+3s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n3n\\;\\le\\; n^2-ns+3s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nns\\;\\le\\; n^2-3n+3s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\;(n-3)\\;\\le\\; n(n-3) .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(n-3\\ge 0\\); dividing by \\(n-3\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n .\n\\]\n\nBut from (2.1) we have \\(s=n-(h+v+d)\\).  Because at least one line of each of the\nthree forbidden directions is needed to cover the three sides (otherwise a side\nwould contain more than one point of a sunny line, impossible), we must have  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ns = n-(h+v+d)\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) contains\nat most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A base configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{h}: y=1\\)   (horizontal);\n* \\(\\ell_{v}: x=1\\)   (vertical);\n* for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the line \\(\\ell_{s}: x+y=s\\).\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence\nnone of them is sunny.  There are exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{h}\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{v}\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\); then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) and\n  \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=s\\), i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_{s}\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and\nall \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny.  Therefore the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Replacing a diagonal line by two sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  \nConsider the two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell^{(1)}_s &: y = -2x + (s+1),\\\\\n\\ell^{(2)}_s &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{s+1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus\nthe two lines are sunny.\n\n**Claim.** The set  \n\n\\[\nD_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\nis the disjoint union of the point sets of \\(\\ell^{(1)}_s\\) and \\(\\ell^{(2)}_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\); then \\(b=s-a\\) with \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\).\n\n*For \\(\\ell^{(1)}_s\\):*  \n\\(b = -2a+(s+1)\\) \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\) \\(s-a = -2a+(s+1)\\) \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\) \\(a=1\\).  \nHence the unique point of \\(D_s\\) with \\(a=1\\), namely \\((1,s-1)\\), lies on\n\\(\\ell^{(1)}_s\\).\n\n*For \\(\\ell^{(2)}_s\\):*  \n\\(b = -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{s+1}{2}\\) \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\) \\(s-a = -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{s+1}{2}\\)  \n\\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\) \\(2(s-a) = -a + (s+1)\\) \\(\\Longleftrightarrow\\) \\(a\\) is even.  \n\nThus every point of \\(D_s\\) with even abscissa satisfies the equation of\n\\(\\ell^{(2)}_s\\); conversely, if \\(a\\) is even the computation above shows that\n\\((a,s-a)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell^{(2)}_s\\).  Hence all points of\n\\(D_s\\) with even \\(a\\) lie on \\(\\ell^{(2)}_s\\).\n\nThe two subsets are disjoint (the only point with \\(a=1\\) has odd abscissa) and\ntheir union is exactly \\(D_s\\). ∎\n\nTherefore the pair \\(\\{\\ell^{(1)}_s,\\ell^{(2)}_s\\}\\) covers precisely the same\npoints of \\(T_n\\) as the original diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nBegin with the base configuration of Section 3 (which yields \\(k=0\\)).  \nWhile at least one diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\) remains, perform the following\noperation:\n\n1. **Delete** the diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\).\n2. **Insert** the two sunny lines \\(\\ell^{(1)}_s,\\ell^{(2)}_s\\) defined in (4.1).\n\nAfter step 1 the family contains \\(n-1\\) lines; after step 2 it contains\n\\(n+1\\) lines, of which the number of sunny lines has increased by two.\nTo restore the total number of lines to \\(n\\) we now **delete one** of the\nremaining non‑sunny lines (any of the two coordinate axes or another diagonal\nline).  This deletion reduces the total back to \\(n\\) and reduces the number\nof non‑sunny lines by one, so the net effect of the whole operation is:\n\n* the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\);\n* the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\n\nBecause the base configuration contains \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, we can repeat\nthe operation at most \\(n-2\\) times.  After performing it \\(k\\) times we obtain\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the claim of Section 4 guarantees that no\n  point is lost when a diagonal line is replaced);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch a family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and that for every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be\nconstructed.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
