al number of\ndistinct points on the union \\(S_1\\cup S_2\\cup S_3\\) is \\(3n-3\\).\n\nA sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of the same side,\notherwise it would be parallel to that side.  Hence a sunny line meets each\nside in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nd=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\\[\ns=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h+v+d+s=n\\).\n\nAll points of the three sides must be covered.  The horizontal lines can\ncover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_1\\); the vertical lines at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(S_2\\); the lines of slope \\(-1\\) at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_3\\); the\n\\(s\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most \\(3s\\) points (one per side).\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n3n-3\\le n(h+v+d)+3s .\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(h+v+d=n-s\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n3n-3\\le n(n-s)+3s\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nns\\le n^2-3n+3s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-3)\\le n\\,(n-3) .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), we may divide by \\(n-3\\) (the case \\(n=3\\) is immediate from\nthe original inequality) and obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=s\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\;(3\\le s\\le n).\n\\]\n\nAll three families are non‑sunny.  There are \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_h\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_v\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\); then \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) and\n  \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=s\\), i.e. it lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and we\nhave exhibited a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal line by two sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  Consider the two lines\n\n\\[\nL^{(1)}_s:\\; y = -2x + (s+1),\\qquad\nL^{(2)}_s:\\; y = \\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{s}{2}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the\nlines are sunny.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \\(D_s = \\bigl(L^{(1)}_s\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl(L^{(2)}_s\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\bigr)\\) and the two sets are disjoint.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA point of \\(D_s\\) can be written \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\).\n\n*For \\(L^{(1)}_s\\):*  \n\n\\[\ns-a = -2a + (s+1)\\iff a=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the unique point \\((1,s-1)\\) of the diagonal belongs to \\(L^{(1)}_s\\).\n\n*For \\(L^{(2)}_s\\):*  \n\n\\[\ns-a = \\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{s}{2}\n\\iff 2s-2a = a + s\n\\iff a = s-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((s-2,2)\\) lies on \\(L^{(2)}_s\\).  Solving the same equation\nfor integer \\(a\\) gives the condition \\(a\\equiv s\\pmod 2\\); consequently all\npoints of the diagonal whose abscissa has the same parity as \\(s\\) satisfy the\nequation of \\(L^{(2)}_s\\).  The remaining points of the diagonal have the\nopposite parity; among them the only one is \\((1,s-1)\\), already accounted for\nby \\(L^{(1)}_s\\).  Therefore the two subsets are disjoint and together equal\n\\(D_s\\). ∎\n\nConsequently the pair \\(\\{L^{(1)}_s, L^{(2)}_s\\}\\) covers exactly the same\nlattice points of \\(T_n\\) as the original diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the configuration of Section 2 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \nWhile there remains at least one diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\), perform the\nfollowing **replacement operation**:\n\n1. Delete the diagonal line \\(\\ell_s\\) (a non‑sunny line).\n2. Insert the two sunny lines \\(L^{(1)}_s, L^{(2)}_s\\) defined in (3.1).\n3. Delete **one** of the remaining non‑sunny lines (any of the two axes or\n   another diagonal line that has not yet been replaced).\n\nAfter step 1 the family contains \\(n-1\\) lines; after step 2 it contains\n\\(n+1\\) lines; after step 3 we are back to exactly \\(n\\) lines.\nDuring the whole process the set of covered lattice points does not change:\nthe deleted diagonal is replaced by the two sunny lines that, by Lemma 2,\ncover precisely the same points, and the extra non‑sunny line removed in step 3\nwas redundant because its points are already covered by the axes or by other\ndiagonals.\n\nEach execution of the operation increases the number of sunny lines by **one**\n(the removed line was non‑sunny, two sunny lines were added).  The base\nconfiguration contains \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, so the operation can be applied\nat most \\(n-2\\) times.  After performing it \\(k\\) times we obtain a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a suitable collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary condition (1.2) and the constructive procedure of Sections 2–4\nshow that the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]"
